\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs20\lang1033 You're Listening to. Cry Or Better Yet Beg. By Solche. Part 1.\par
\par
\par
\par
Prologue.\par
\par
\par
Orphaned at a young age, Layla Llewellyn feels like the luckiest girl in the world after she moves in with her uncle Bill, a gardener who lives on the scenic Arvis estate in the Berg Empire. To Layla, Arvis seems like paradise; she loves to explore the vast forest, always bringing her notebook to record the wildlife she encounters. She especially loves the birds, watching them with awe as they hatch and grow from fluffy chicks into beautiful birds. Duke Herhardt, the young, handsome lord of Arvis, is also interested in birds\f1\u8213?and in Layla. But the difference is, he\rquote s interested in birds because he likes to hunt them\'85 and he\rquote s interested in Layla because he likes to make her cry. If a lovely bird is trapped in a golden cage, which will she choose: a life of luxury, or freedom?\par
\par
\par
\par
1.\par
\par
A Delivered Girl.\par
\par
The girl showed up one early-spring day, riding in the mail coach. It was late afternoon, and Bill Remmer was busy planting rose seedlings.\par
\ldblquote Are you Mr. Bill Remmer?\rdblquote  the girl asked cautiously. Bill stared back at her in a daze. Her pronunciation was very smooth, but there was something off about her accent.\par
\ldblquote Yeah, I\rquote m Bill Remmer,\rdblquote  he replied.\par
The girl watched as Bill brushed the dirt off his hands and took off his straw hat. She flinched when she saw his tan face, no longer obscured by the shadow from the wide brim of his hat.\par
This reaction was nothing new to Bill. Most people reacted similarly when first seeing his rugged features and large build.\par
\ldblquote Who are you?\rdblquote  he asked, frowning. The frown made him look even more threatening.\par
\par
\ldblquote Hello, Uncle Bill. My name is Layla Llewellyn. I come from Lovita.\rdblquote  She spoke clearly and slowly.\par
Lovita\'85\par
Bill now realized why she spoke with a strange accent. \ldblquote Did you cross the border and come all the way to Berg by yourself?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes. I took the train,\rdblquote  she replied. Smiling awkwardly, she straightened her posture to an unnatural degree.\par
The mailman who had brought her walked up to them. \ldblquote Ah, I see she\rquote s already met you, Mr. Remmer.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Good timing,\rdblquote  Bill replied. \ldblquote Why did you bring her here?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I saw her walking with her luggage all alone in front of the station. I asked her where she was headed, and she said she was on her way to find Mr. Bill Remmer, the gardener for the Herhardt family. I was on my way here to deliver mail anyway, so I brought her along,\rdblquote  the mailman replied with a smile. He then handed Bill a letter. The envelope showed that the letter was from distant relatives of Bill\rquote s who lived in the neighboring country of Lovita.\par
Bill immediately tore open the envelope and started reading the letter. It told the story of an orphaned child who had been taken in by a series of relatives, all of whom were too poor to able to continue fostering her. The child\rquote s name was Layla Llewellyn. Thus, it seemed that the little girl standing in front of him was the orphan mentioned in the letter. He let out a bewildered chuckle and grumbled. \ldblquote Damn people. This news sure traveled fast.\rdblquote\par
None of this little orphan\rquote s relatives in Lovita could foster her. Even though Bill was only distantly related to her, his circumstances were better than any of theirs, so they had sent her to him. However, they had added that if his situation did not allow him to raise her, he could leave her at an orphanage.\par
\par
\ldblquote These people can drop dead. No matter what things were like, how could they send that little girl all the way here by herself?\rdblquote  he muttered, crumpling up the letter and throwing it to the ground. Now that he had grasped the whole situation, his face grew red with anger. She had been passed from family to family like a hot potato, until there were no more families left to take her in. Then, she had simply been handed the contact details of a distant relative living in a foreign country, and sent off across the border to him. It was like she was being banished from her home country.\par
Just then, the girl, who had been watching Bill quietly, said, \ldblquote Excuse me, Uncle Bill. I\rquote m not actually that young. I\rquote ll be twelve in a few weeks.\rdblquote  She was being careful to speak in as mature a manner as she could, and had slightly raised her heels off the ground to make herself appear taller.\par
Even more bewildered than before, Bill let out another chuckle. She was so small, he had first guessed her age to be around ten. He was happy at least to learn that she was older than his guess.\par
Eventually, the mailman who had delivered the troublesome girl left, leaving the two alone in the garden. Bill put his head in his hands and asked God for guidance. While he was technically related to the girl\rquote s late father, he hadn\rquote t seen him in over twenty years. To think, this daughter of a distant relative was left with him to raise her. This tiny little girl with the widower Bill Remmer!\par
Although the early-spring weather was still quite chilly, the girl was wearing only a flimsy outfit. She also looked thin as a rail. The only other aspects of her appearance that stood out were her big, green eyes, and her blonde hair, which looked like it was made of gold thread. Bill came to the firm conclusion that he could not take care of her.\par
\par
It then occurred to him that the only other option was to place her in an orphanage, the thought of which upset him just as much. He once again quietly cursed the relatives that had caused this mess. The girl flinched, but maintained a brave facial expression. However, she was unable to hide her quivering hands, or her lower lip, which had grown red because she had been chewing on it.\par
\ldblquote Follow me,\rdblquote  he said, shaking his head as he started to walk. \ldblquote Let\rquote s eat first, and then I can think things through.\rdblquote  His curt words were carried away by the evening breeze.\par
Layla, who had been standing in the same spot since she arrived, started following behind him. At first, she walked slowly and tentatively, but gradually her steps became light and joyful.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\ldblquote Is that all you\rquote re going to eat?\rdblquote  Bill asked, frowning at the small portion the girl had dished onto her plate.\par
\ldblquote Yes. I only eat a little. Really,\rdblquote  she replied, smiling.\par
Bill grew slightly more frustrated than he already was. \ldblquote Listen, kiddo, I hate kids with small appetites.\rdblquote\par
The girl\rquote s eyes widened at this harsh comment. She had pulled up her sleeves, exposing her slender wrists to the light shining down from the table lamp.\par
\ldblquote No matter what you\rquote re served, you should eat lots, like a cow,\rdblquote  he said. His face grew more stern.\par
\par
For a moment she stared into space, blinking slowly as she considered his words. Then she took another piece of meat and slice of bread and placed them on her plate, then started scarfing them down voraciously, making it seem like she had been quite hungry after all. \ldblquote Maybe I can\rquote t quite eat like a cow, but I am a good eater, Uncle,\rdblquote  she said, smiling at him with her breadcrumb-covered lips.\par
\ldblquote Yes, I can see that clearly now,\rdblquote  he replied, chuckling as he took a sip of liquor. \ldblquote Aren\rquote t you afraid of me?\rdblquote  he then asked, intentionally making an intense grimace as he looked at her. However, she simply stared right back at him, not flinching or avoiding his gaze for a moment.\par
\ldblquote Nope,\rdblquote  she replied. \ldblquote You never yell at me. You gave me all this delicious food. I\rquote m grateful for those things. You seem like a good person.\rdblquote\par
What kind of life was she living before this, that such simple things make her grateful? he wondered. This gave him a bitter taste in his mouth, so he stood up and poured himself a large glass of beer.\par
The letter had stated that the girl\rquote s mother had run away with another man, abandoning her daughter and her father. Her father had been so distraught by this that he became an alcoholic and ended up dying of alcohol poisoning. After that, the girl had been passed from relative\rquote s home to relative\rquote s home.\par
Pondering over all this, Bill realized that her life had been far from easy. Even so, he still thought it was out of the question for him to be the one to raise her. As he swigged his beer, he resolved to make a decision on what to do with the girl by the following week.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\ldblquote Did you all hear? Mr. Remmer, the gardener, has started taking care of a young girl!\rdblquote  exclaimed a young maid as she rushed into the house staff\rquote s break room. Everyone in the break room turned to face her.\par
\ldblquote A girl? Mr. Remmer? I\rquote d sooner believe that he was taking care of a lion, or an elephant,\rdblquote  a servant said, snorting.\par
Bill Remmer, the gardener for the House of Herhardt, was a man with an innate talent for growing flowers. Thanks to this talent, he had maintained his job for twenty years already, despite his short temper and unsociable personality. He was so even-handed in his treatment of everyone that he acted the same way with the Herhardt family as he did with his coworkers.\par
Even so, the family trusted him deeply. And none of them so much as Dowager Herhardt did. She had an eccentric love of flowers, so she had limitless understanding and tolerance when it came to anything connected to her garden. Even the cottage that Bill had been provided in the woods behind the mansion had been her idea.\par
Bill lived a simple life. He worked in the garden, and rested in his cottage. Other than having the occasional drink with his coworkers, he spent most of his time in the company of flowers and trees. It had been over a decade since his wife had passed away from an illness, and yet he rarely saw other women. And now that stone-faced man was taking care of a young girl?\par
Just as everyone in the break room agreed that the rumor had to be false, a maid standing by the window shouted, \ldblquote Oh my, looks like it\rquote s true! Take a look\par
\par
over there.\rdblquote  She pointed through window as she watched with widened eyes.\par
Everyone rushed to the window. As soon as they saw what the maid was pointing at, they reacted with just as much surprise as she had. Bill was on the far side of the garden, doing his work in his usual, hunched-over posture. And following right behind him was a little girl, just like the rumor. Her golden hair, which was woven into a braid, swung like a pendulum behind her with each step.\par
\ldblquote I still haven\rquote t made up my mind,\rdblquote  Bill repeated each time anyone asked him about the girl. \ldblquote She can\rquote t stay here, so I\rquote ll have to think of another solution.\rdblquote  As he continued to delay making up his mind, spring turned into summer.\par
Meanwhile, Layla Llewellyn gradually became a part of the household, and the staff grew used to seeing her busily roaming the gardens and forests of the estate.\par
\ldblquote Looks like she\rquote s grown a bit,\rdblquote  Chef Mona remarked, laughing to herself as she glanced out the window.\par
Layla was strolling through the woods behind the cottage, looking at all the various herbs and flowers growing there.\par
\ldblquote She still has a long way to go,\rdblquote  Bill replied. \ldblquote She\rquote s still small for her age.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Come on, Bill. Raising kids isn\rquote t the same as growing plants. They don\rquote t\par
shoot up to full height all of a sudden one morning,\rdblquote  Mona replied, shaking her head as she set her basket down on the table.\par
\ldblquote What\rquote s in there?\rdblquote  Bill asked. \ldblquote Cookies and cake,\rdblquote  she replied.\par
\ldblquote There was a tea party at the mansion yesterday.\rdblquote  \ldblquote I hate sweet things.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote So what? These are for Layla,\rdblquote  Mona casually replied.\par
\par
Bill\rquote s dark brow furrowed at this. The girl\rquote s stay there was supposed to be temporary, and yet somewhere along the way, the household staff had started taking care of her. They asked Bill how she was doing, brought food for her, and sometimes even went to visit her. This bothered Bill.\par
\ldblquote You should buy her some new clothes. If she grows any more, her skirt won\rquote t reach down to her knees,\rdblquote  said Mona, clicking her tongue as she watched Layla chasing after a bird.\par
Bill couldn\rquote t dispute her point. He knew nothing about children, and yet even he could clearly see that her clothes didn\rquote t fit her anymore.\par
Just when Mona was about to leave, she glanced towards the window again and gasped. Running to the window, she shouted, \ldblquote Oh my! My goodness! Look at her!\rdblquote  Bill calmly glanced in the direction she was pointing.\par
The bird Layla was chasing had landed on a tree branch, and Layla had immediately started climbing swiftly up the tree, moving as nimbly and effortlessly as a squirrel.\par
\ldblquote Yep, she\rquote s sure got a knack for climbing trees,\rdblquote  Bill flatly remarked.\par
Mona glared at him. \ldblquote Bill! You knew she liked to climb trees, and you just let her do it? Is this your idea of how to raise a child?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote As you can see, she\rquote s growing up to be nice and strong.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You\rquote re raising that girl to be a tomboy! My goodness,\rdblquote  Mona loudly complained.\par
However, Bill just kept peeking out the window, barely listening. Layla was perched on a tree branch, watching the birds flutter about the treetops.\par
During the past few months while she had been staying at the Herhardt estate, it had become clear that Layla was full of curiosity about the world around her. Flowers and herbs, birds and insects\emdash  whatever caught her eye would pique her curiosity and her wonder.\par
\par
One day, when she hadn\rquote t returned for dinner, Bill had gone into the forest looking for her. He had found her sitting by the river, gazing at a flock of waterfowl. She had been so captivated by sight; she hadn\rquote t noticed him calling her name repeatedly.\par
Mona spent awhile longer stridently lecturing Bill, then finally left. Bill shook his head to clear the echoing sound of Mona\rquote s scolding from his ears, then he sauntered to the area behind his cottage. As soon as Layla saw him, she waved at him and gleefully shouted, \ldblquote Uncle!\rdblquote\par
She then climbed down the tree just as quickly as she had climbed up it, and zipped over to Bill, stopping right in front of him. She was dressed in a drab, gray dress. Not only was the skirt too short for her, but the sleeves were also too. Realizing that it would be improper for her to meet Duke Herhardt while wearing hand-me-down clothing, Bill decided that he would have to buy her a new outfit after all.\par
Once they arrived at the cottage\rquote s back door, he turned to her and impulsively said, \ldblquote Go in and get ready. I\rquote ll be waiting for you out here.\rdblquote\par
She looked bewildered for a moment, then a fearful expression flashed across her face as she asked, \ldblquote B-but, why, Uncle?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t look so scared. We\rquote re just going into town to buy you some clothes.\rdblquote  He cleared his throat and scratched the back of his neck, then added, \ldblquote Duke Herhardt will be arriving soon. You can\rquote t meet him looking like that.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote The duke? That\rquote s the owner of this estate, right?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote That\rquote s right. It\rquote s summer break now, so he\rquote ll be returning home.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Summer break? Does the duke go to school?\rdblquote  Layla asked, tilting her head and squinting her eyes.\par
Bill laughed and stroked her disheveled hair. \ldblquote The duke is only eighteen years old. He has no choice but to go to school.\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote What?! He\rquote s eighteen? The duke?\rdblquote\par
Bill found her reaction so cute, he started laughing louder than before. He stroked her hair again. Against his rough fingertips, her hair felt as soft as cotton.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
A train from the capital arrived at the platform at Karlsbar Station. The attendants that had been waiting all walked in perfect order to the door of the first-class car, where they lined up and stood at attention. Soon, a tall, slim boy descended from the train onto the platform.\par
\ldblquote Hello, Master,\rdblquote  said Hessen, the butler. After his polite greeting, he and all the rest of the attendants bowed their heads respectfully. Standing with an elegant posture, Matthias gave a slight nod in recognition of their greeting. His red lips were smiling in a subtle but not unfriendly way.\par
The attendants from the House of Herhardt only started moving once Matthias had taken a few strides forward. The crowd of onlookers quickly parted to let them pass through. Matthias walked the length of the platform at an unyielding brisk pace. When he exited the station and saw the carriage waiting for him out front, he snickered and said, \ldblquote Oh my, a carriage.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Ah\'85 yes, Master. Dowager Herhardt doesn\rquote t trust cars.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I know. For Grandmother, cars are no more than vulgar, dangerous hunks of metal.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I apologize. Next time I\rquote ll-\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote No. I don\rquote t mind doing things the classic way now and then.\rdblquote  Matthias then calmly boarded the carriage. His long arms and legs, which were still a bit lanky at his young age, moved in a smooth, unhurried manner.\par
Once he was aboard, the carriage started moving, quickly picking up speed until the horses were galloping. After passing through the city square and the busy shopping district, it grew relatively quiet in the carriage. A separate carriage carrying Matthias\rquote s luggage followed a moderate distance behind, its golden crest gleaming in the sunlight.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
2.\par
\par
The Slayer of Beautiful Birds.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
Matthias sat deep in his seat, staring out the carriage window, while his butler Hessen thoroughly briefed him on all his family\rquote s pending\par
issues. These issues included an upcoming visit from some relatives who were going to be spending the summer in Arvis, several social gatherings, and insurance issues for the merchant vessel that would be setting sail the following month. Matthias showed he was listening by responding with brief replies or nods.\par
The business was run by the directors, while family affairs were handled by the two ladies of the house, Matthias\rquote s mother and grandmother. However, final decisions always fell to Matthias himself. This was a responsibility that he had held since the age of twelve. Hessen finished his report just as they reached Platanus Road, which led to the Herhardt estate.\par
\par
Matthias was sitting with his head tilted, gazing at the familiar scenery. The road was lined on either side with tall trees that arched towards each other, as if holding hands. The sunlight dappled the road with a beautiful pattern as it flowed through the fluttering leaves.\par
Once they entered the estate, a white mansion with a deep-blue roof came into view. Matthias\rquote s mother and grandmother were standing in front of the mansion, ready to receive the head of the household. As Matthias straightened his already-straight tie, the carriage came to a stop and the door opened.\par
\ldblquote Welcome home, Matthias,\rdblquote  said Dowager Katharina von Herhardt, smiling brightly as she greeted her grandson. Matthias bowed his head and accepted a kiss from his grandmother. His mother, Elise von Herhardt, greeted him in a more direct manner, hugging him lightly as she smiled and said, \ldblquote You\rquote ve grown, I see.\rdblquote  Her black hair, which matched that of her son, gleamed in the early-summer sun.\par
Matthias looked back at her with the same smile. He then offered similar greetings to the servants lined up to see him. He effortlessly displayed polished decorum and proper etiquette in his every word and action. In moments like this, the question of whether he was a man or still just a boy became moot. He was simply Duke Herhardt, the perfect head of this household.\par
With his mother and grandmother walking on either side of him, he took the lead and crossed through the mansion\rquote s main hall. Before climbing the stairs, he glanced up and looked at the huge chandelier, which was kept lit even during the day. Beyond that, he saw the Herhardt family crest imprinted on the ceiling.\par
He was a Herhardt.\par
The family name was associated with cool-headed intellect, gracious demeanor,  and  self-possession.  Matthias  had  never  had  any  doubts  or\par
\par
complaints about the life he led as Duke Herhardt. He knew exactly what this life required of him, and he accepted it readily. It was as natural to him as breathing, and as easy.\par
Lowering his gaze again, he ascended the stairs with large steps.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The servants were finally able to breathe easy now that their master had entered the mansion.\par
All of Arvis had been bustling with activity for days as everyone prepared to receive Duke Herhardt. Everything had to be perfect on the day he arrived, including the household staff. Even the low-ranking servants that the duke would never lay eyes on had a duty to carry themselves with grace and dignity. Layla Llewellyn, Arvis\rquote s uninvited guest, was no exception. \ldblquote Did the duke arrive? Already?\rdblquote  she muttered in a slightly disappointed tone, standing near a group of servants. The skirt of the white dress Bill had bought for her fluttered\par
as she moved.\par
\ldblquote You\rquote ll be able to see him plenty in the woods. I\rquote ll have to ask his permission at that time,\rdblquote  Bill bluntly replied. He then began walking. Layla followed behind him, practically running to keep up. \ldblquote Does the duke like the woods too?\rdblquote  she asked.\par
\ldblquote Sure he does. He likes hunting.\rdblquote\par
Layla\rquote s eyes widened. \ldblquote Hunting? In the woods?\rdblquote  Bill snorted and looked down at the girl. \ldblquote Isn\rquote t it natural that the woods would be the family\rquote s hunting ground?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Then\'85 does he hunt birds too?\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote I think bird hunting is the duke\rquote s favorite,\rdblquote  Bill casually replied. Layla suddenly stopped walking, shocked by this remark.\par
Once Bill realized the reason for her reaction, he awkwardly cleared his throat. He thought about telling a lie to comfort her, but he knew it was no use. If he gave her false comfort now, she would be all the more shocked when the duke arrived at the hunting ground within the next few days.\par
Feeling like he needed to say something to comfort her, he said, \ldblquote You\rquote ll be amazed when you see the duke\rquote s shooting skills. He\rquote s young, but he\rquote s an amazing marksman.\rdblquote  As he listened to himself, he realized that this comment might not have been as comforting to her as he had intended. Looking down, he saw that she was already on the verge of tears.\par
\ldblquote Why does he hunt birds?\rdblquote  she asked, There\rquote s already so much food in the mansion\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Hunting is a recreational activity for nobles. Of all the types of game they hunt, birds and the most fun, and\'85\rdblquote  Bill caught himself, realizing he had once again said something he shouldn\rquote t have.\par
Layla was staring at him with a shocked expression on her face. Bill started to get frustrated. He didn\rquote t like that he needed to be so careful about what he said around this little girl, explaining every last thing to her in order to protect her feelings.\par
He almost shouted, Why do you like birds so much?! Ultimately, though, he decided to remain silent. He knew that one more word from him could send her into a fit of crying, and he didn\rquote t want to deal with that. He hated crying children. After hesitating for a moment, he continued walking.\par
Her shoulders slumped, Layla followed behind him with feeble steps. The excitement she had been displaying about her new dress a moment earlier was gone. The way she had been joyfully prancing about had been quite a sight to\par
\par
see. After a long silence, she looked earnestly up at Bill and cautiously said, \ldblquote I hope the duke stops enjoying hunting. Do you think that could happen?\rdblquote\par
Unsure what to say in response, Bill simply scratched the back of his neck.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
Layla began to think that her prayers had been answered. It had been a week since the duke had returned, and he still hadn\rquote t visited the hunting ground. This was only natural, given the many guests that had flocked to the mansion to see him.\par
There were boisterous parties every day in the mansion, but the woods remained quiet. Summer was now in full swing. The eggs that mother birds had been brooding with care were now hatched. The wild rosebuds had blossomed and were now in full bloom. Layla frequently wandered through the forest, overjoyed with all the small changes that were on display.\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t go too far, Layla!\rdblquote  Bill shouted one day as Layla once again left the cottage to go exploring. \ldblquote I won\rquote t! I\rquote m just going to the river for a bit! I\rquote ll see you later, Uncle!\rdblquote  she replied. She then turned and hurried off into the woods, her arms flailing about as she ran. The old leather bag that she had slung over her shoulder bounced with each step she took.\par
Once she was deep in the woods, the first thing she looked at was a nest of chickadee hatchlings on a tree branch. The featherless chicks were waiting for their mother to return with their food. She scuttled down the tree and jotted down a description of the baby birds in a small notebook that she kept in her bag. She also tried sketching them as best she could.\par
\par
She had gotten into the habit of recording everything she saw in the woods in her notebook. The land she was on was more beautiful than anywhere else she had ever been, and she wanted to remember everything. Whenever the day came when she would have to leave the estate, she would be able to open her notebook and enjoy everything she had seen again. This lessened her sadness at the prospect of having to leave.\par
As she walked along the path leading to the river, she kept a steady record of all the forest sights. She placed some pretty colored flower petals between the pages of her notebook, and picked and ate some raspberries from the side of the path. The sun was right overhead when she arrived at the shimmering riverside.\par
She climbed up a huge tree that stood at the boundary between the forest and the river. This was her favorite tree. Its long branches were so thick, that they were as comfortable to sit on as chairs. Just as she was opening her notebook, she heard the faint sound of horse hooves coming from somewhere beyond the river.\par
She quickly put her notebook safely back into her bag. Meanwhile, the galloping sound had grown closer. She was scared. She held her breath and hugged the trunk of the tree. A short while later, a horse with sleek brown hair appeared. A man was riding on its back. He came to a stop underneath the very tree that Layla had climbed. His motions were smooth and nimble as he got down from the horse.\par
Layla felt like climbing down from the tree, but the unfamiliar man was now leaning against it. She felt flustered, unsure of what would be the proper thing to say to him. Meanwhile, the man removed his hat. Just then, the bag she had slung over her shoulder bumped into the branch, making a loud sound. Her memory of what happened next was hazy.\par
\par
The man reflexively looked up, and Layla saw his face. His blue eyes were like clear marbles, visible through the black hair flowing down over his forehead. By the time Layla had gathered her wits, he had drawn his rifle and now had it pointed at her. The long barrel of the rifle shone menacingly at her. She remained motionless, still hugging the tree tightly. Her whole body was quivering. As the man watched her, he let out a sigh and slowly lowered his hunting rifle. Twisting his lips, he then asked in a low voice, \ldblquote Who are\par
you?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m Layla\'85\rdblquote  she replied, barely managing to speak without breaking into sobs. Her golden hair fluttered in the wind blowing from the river.\par
His eyes narrowed. \ldblquote What?\rdblquote\par
Holding onto the tree so tightly that her hands were hurting, she replied, \ldblquote Layla. Layla Llewellyn.\rdblquote\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\ldblquote Uncle! Uncle Bill! Uncle!\rdblquote  The sound of Layla\rquote s breathless cries echoed through the woods as she called to Bill.\par
Bill was sitting in front of his shed, sharpening the blade of one of his gardening tools. When he heard her desperate cries, he turned his head in bewilderment, only to see her frantically running towards him, her face beet- red. \ldblquote What\rquote s the matter?\rdblquote  he asked.\par
Without waiting to catch her breath, she quickly replied, \ldblquote There\rquote s someone in the woods! A tall man!\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote Sounds like you met the duke while he was out on a hunt,\rdblquote  Bill casually replied as he picked up the next tool to sharpen.\par
\ldblquote He had black hair, and blue eyes, his voice was as smooth as a duck\rquote s feathers.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No doubt about it, that was Duke Herhardt,\rdblquote  Bill replied, chuckling.\par
Layla stood in place for a while, trying to catch her breath. The good- looking but scary man had stared at her for a while, then turned away without a word. While he was getting back onto his horse, two men who seemed to be his companions had appeared. The man had joined the other men, and the three had gone together further into the woods. Once they were fully out of sight, Layla had scuttled down the tree and fled to the cottage.\par
\ldblquote That means that the duke is\'85\rdblquote  Before Layla could finish her sentence, a loud gunshot rang out, disturbing the stillness of the forest. Startled, Layla turned towards the source of the noise.\par
On the far side of the forest, a bunch of startled birds were flying upward. One of them struggled to ascend, and soon fell down amid the trees. A few more shots rang out.\par
Bill set down his work and stood up. Layla was frozen stiff, staring at the far side of the forest.\par
\ldblquote Layla,\rdblquote  he said, patting her on the shoulder. After a long delay, she finally looked up at him. At that moment, Bill found himself holding his breath.\par
\par
The little girl was crying.\par
