The sun was hot, but the shady area under the trees was breezy and cool. Layla was sitting on a blanket, with her arms wrapped around her knees.\par
Bill and the other gardeners were busy digging up a wilted rose bush and planting late- blooming rose blossoms in the ground.\par
The garden behind the mansion seemed to have just about every rose variety in the world growing in it. The rose was the national flower of the Berg Empire, and was also the favorite flower of the duke\rquote s mother and grandmother.\par
Earlier that day, after hearing more gunshots echoing from the woods, Layla had felt too agitated to sit still, so she had been anxiously pacing around inside the cottage. When Bill had seen that, he had offered her his hand. Layla had reflexively put her hand in his. He had then taken her out into the garden\par
\par
with him. As the sound of the gunshots had faded away, she had finally calmed down.\par
Is it really okay for me to lounge around like this while Uncle Bill is working in the hot sun? she thought to herself, looking awkwardly at Bill. She then let out a small sigh and closed her eyes.\par
Bill had told her that he would be in trouble if she tried to help him with his work. He had said that he hated children who didn\rquote t do as they were told. Thus, she had no choice but to sit and wait for him to finish, even though she felt guilty.\par
After reluctantly coming to this conclusion, she opened her eyes again and was startled by what she saw. An unfamiliar boy was standing right in front of her. He was well dressed and looked to be about the same age as her.\par
As soon as they made eye contact, he smiled and said, \ldblquote Hello.\rdblquote  He had blond hair and an attractive smile. He glanced around, then solemnly asked, \ldblquote Do you live here?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Yeah. I live with Bill,\rdblquote  she replied, looking at him with narrowed eyes.\par
\ldblquote Bill? You mean that scary gardener guy?\rdblquote  \ldblquote He\rquote s not scary.\rdblquote\par
The boy tilted his head. \ldblquote Really? He seems scary to me.\rdblquote  He casually sat down next to Layla.Layla still wasn\rquote t sure if she could trust the boy. \ldblquote Do you live here too?\rdblquote  she asked.\par
The boy shook his head, chuckling. \ldblquote No. I came with my dad. He\rquote s the Herhardts\rquote  family doctor. He came today to give Dowager Herhardt a check- up. Sometimes I come along when he does that. Dowager Herhardt said it was all right.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I see,\rdblquote  Layla replied. \ldblquote How old are you?\rdblquote  the boy asked. \ldblquote Twelve.\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote Same as me. But you\rquote re so small.\rdblquote  The boy burst into laughter. Layla started blushing slightly. \ldblquote You\rquote re small too,\rdblquote  she said. \ldblquote Well, I\rquote m the tallest kid in my class,\rdblquote  the boy replied, sitting up as straight as he could. Sure enough, he did look to be taller than the average boy his age.\par
\ldblquote Fine, but\'85 you\rquote re not as tall as Uncle Bill,\rdblquote  Layla muttered, sounding less confident than before.\par
The boy stared at her for a moment, then laughed again. He seemed to laugh a lot. \ldblquote Come on, Bill\rquote s a tall guy. No kids are taller than him. I bet there aren\rquote t even any grown-ups taller than him.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m not so sure about that,\rdblquote  Layla replied. Feeling awkward now, she grabbed a handful of grass from the ground next to the blanket, which stained her fingers green. She hoped that the boy would leave soon, but he showed no indication that he would do so. Noticing a peach that she had earlier placed on the edge of the blanket, she impulsively asked, \ldblquote Want some?\rdblquote\par
The boy nodded eagerly. It seemed that he not only laughed easily, but was a generally bold, outgoing person. Layla retrieved a pocket knife from her bag and started cutting up the peach. The boy started snickering. \ldblquote You\rquote re really funny. I can\rquote t believe a girl would have a knife in her bag.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Uncle Bill gave it to me. Don\rquote t tease me,\rdblquote  she replied, scrunching her nose at the boy. She then handed him a peach half. The sweet smell of the peach\rquote s flesh wafted in the space between them.\par
\ldblquote Why do you look so downcast?\rdblquote  he asked, after finishing his half of the peach. \ldblquote Is something the matter?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote The duke and his friends keep hunting birds,\rdblquote  she gloomily replied. The boy looked puzzled. \ldblquote What\rquote s wrong with that?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote They\rquote re killing birds for fun.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Isn\rquote t that the whole point of hunting? To kill things for fun?\rdblquote\par
\par
She raised her green eyes and stared solemnly at him. \ldblquote Do you do that too?\rdblquote\par
The boy looked like he would barely be able to lift a hunting rifle.\par
\ldblquote Uh\'85 well\'85 no,\rdblquote  he replied. After a moment, he shook his head vigorously and firmly said, \ldblquote No, I don\rquote t. I feel sorry\rquote  for the poor animals.\rdblquote  Hearing this, the sullen look on Layla\rquote s face slowly transformed into a smile. In a cheery tone, she asked, \ldblquote Want another peach?\rdblquote  Once again, the boy nodded eagerly. Layla cut the second peach very unevenly, and gave the larger half to the boy. The boy\rquote s cheeks reddened slightly. He seemed slightly uncomfortable in his semi-formal clothes. Suddenly, a voice could be heard in the distance. \ldblquote Kyle! Kyle!\rdblquote\par
The boy, who had already eaten his second piece of peach and was now fiddling with the pit, bolted up as soon as he heard the voice. \ldblquote I have to go now.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote All right. Bye.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Kyle Ettman,\rdblquote  the boy said, extending his hand towards her. \ldblquote That\rquote s my name. What\rquote s yours?\rdblquote  \ldblquote I\rquote m Layla. Layla Llewellyn,\rdblquote  she replied, clumsily accepting his hand in hers.\par
The two children exchanged a serious handshake, despite the fact that their hands were sticky with peach juice. \ldblquote Bye, Layla. I\rquote ll see you later. Next time, I\rquote ll bring the snack! \rdblquote  he shouted, looking back at her as he ran off.\par
Layla thought about telling him that they might not see each other again because she might be moving away soon, but then she changed her mind and just waved meekly at him. She didn\rquote t like talking about that possibility out loud. Doing so always filled her with anxiety.\par
Now that Kyle had left, Layla\rquote s world was once again peaceful. She sat in the rose- scented breeze, waiting for Bill to finish his work. Eventually, she\par
\par
dozed off. When she heard Bill calling her name, she cracked her eyes open and saw that the long summer day was already coming to an end.\par
She jumped to her feet, grabbed her bag, and picked up the blanket. She scampered over to Bill, looking much more carefree than she had been earlier. \ldblquote Uncle, I just met this kid, and\'85\rdblquote  Before she could finish telling him about the boy with whom she had shared the peaches, she saw the silhouettes of people walking out of the far side of the woods. She froze.\par
It was Duke Herhardt and his friends.\par
Matthias paused in the middle of the rose garden. He saw the gruff gardener Bill Remmer bowing his head towards him. Shortly thereafter, he noticed that there was a small child hiding behind him. \ldblquote It\rquote s been a long time, Mr. Remmer,\rdblquote  he said, nodding back at him. The rest of the hunting group stopped a moderate distance behind Matthias.\par
\ldblquote This kid is going to be staying here at Arvis for the time being,\rdblquote  Bill said, with an awkward look on his face. He then patted Layla on the back, and she tentatively stepped out into the open.\par
When Matthias saw her shimmering blonde hair, he recognized her immediately. It was the girl that had been up in the tree, whom he had mistaken for a bird and almost shot.\par
\ldblquote I already got permission from Lady Elise and Dowager Herhardt, but I should have asked you as well,\rdblquote  Bill said, bowing his head again. Layla, who was standing next to him, followed his lead and bowed as well.\par
Matthias slowly looked down. When he made eye contact with the girl, she flinched, but didn\rquote t look away. He saw her narrowed eyes and her tightly shut lips. It was the same expression she had looked at him with when they had first met in the woods.\par
\par
\ldblquote You\rquote re that girl. The girl who lives in the woods,\rdblquote  said Matthias\rquote s cousin Riette, chuckling.\par
Layla started blushing and hid behind Bill again. She was acting the same way she did whenever she saw the hunting group in the woods. She always glared at them, then hid behind a tree. Once they finished hunting, she would find the dead birds and bury them, sobbing the whole time.\par
\ldblquote Ah, yes, that\rquote s fine, Mr. Remmer,\rdblquote  Matthias said, smiling. He didn\rquote t care if the gardener wanted to take care of a child. \ldblquote Thank you, Your Grace,\rdblquote  Bill replied, bowing once again. Matthias gave the slightest nod in return, then started walking again.\par
Only once they started to leave did Layla lift her head. The duke\rquote s relatives and friends were following behind him. Because of the hot weather, they had all taken off their coats and rolled up their sleeves. Only Duke Herhardt himself had kept on his complete hunting outfit. The only exception was his cap, which he had removed and was carrying in his hand.\par
There were servants following behind the main group. Layla watched them intently. She backed away in shock when she saw that they were carrying not only the hunting rifles, but also a number of animal carcasses. The sharp smell of blood filled her nostrils. She hunched her shoulders and shut her eyes.\par
Noticing that she was quivering, Bill patted her on the shoulder. It was an awkward but kindhearted gesture.\par
