The maid brought Layla into an unfamiliar world, a world where glamorous people dressed in bright, candy-colored clothing were sitting under a white awning that fluttered in the breeze.\par
\ldblquote Oh my, what a cute little girl,\rdblquote  said an incredibly beautiful noblewoman. Turning from Layla to the brown-haired girl sitting there, she asked, \ldblquote What do you think, Claudine? Do you like her?\rdblquote\par
The girl who had just been addressed as \ldblquote Claudine\rdblquote  smiled and nodded, saying, \ldblquote Thank you, Lady Herhardt.\rdblquote\par
Layla started to feel dizzy amidst this conversation that she didn\rquote t understand. She desperately wanted to return to Uncle Bill\rquote s cottage, but no one was showing any interest in how she felt. A noblewoman who Layla guessed was the duke\rquote s mother said something to a maid.\par
\par
The maid who had brought Layla from the garden passed her off to the other maid, who then led her away. Layla still had no idea what was going on. Finding herself in the first luxurious bathroom she had ever seen, Layla took a bath, and then put on an amazingly soft, white dress that she had been provided. As the maid combed her tangled hair, it hurt a lot, but she bit her lip and endured it. She was worried that if she complained or got upset, it could spell trouble for Uncle Bill.\par
\ldblquote Miss Claudine is Count Brandt\rquote s daughter. You\rquote d better behave yourself in front of her. Got it?\rdblquote  warned the maid who had brought Layla up to the mansion\rquote s second floor.\par
Layla nodded reflexively. The maid then slowly opened door to the drawing-room. Claudine greeted them, acting as adult like as she could. \ldblquote Hello. What\rquote s your name? How old are you?\rdblquote  She lowered her head and looked into Layla\rquote s eyes.\par
\ldblquote My name is Layla Llewellyn, Miss. I\rquote m twelve years old.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Really? You\rquote re so small, I thought you were younger than me.\rdblquote\par
This was Layla\rquote s least favorite thing to hear, but she kept silent. She repeated the phrase For Uncle Bill over and over again in her mind. This gave her extra patience. Claudine seemed not to think it necessary to introduce herself. She simply turned and started walking away. Layla stiffly followed behind her.\par
Claudine suggested several activities for the two of them to partake in, including playing piano, singing songs, and making paper flowers. Layla shook her head. She didn\rquote t know how to do any of those things. Claudine gave her some more options, including dice games, word games, and chess. Layla didn\rquote t know how to play those games either.\par
\par
Claudine looked back and forth between the toy-covered table and Layla. \ldblquote Poor you,\rdblquote  she said, smiling tenderly. She then let out a disappointed sigh and slowly stood up. Layla felt an extreme sense of helplessness as she regarded the unfamiliar items on the table. Claudine walked in front of Layla\rquote s chair and lamented, \ldblquote You don\rquote t know a thing.\rdblquote\par
Layla could tell that Claudine was trying to speak as gently as she could, without letting her frustration or irritation through. This only made Layla feel more humiliated. She felt like she should respond, but she didn\rquote t know what the proper thing to say was in this situation.\par
Luckily, Claudine simply turned away from her without waiting for a response. Before exiting the room, she sighed and muttered, \ldblquote I can\rquote t believe it. She\rquote s no better than a puppy.\rdblquote\par
Layla was left by herself in the lavish drawing-room. She felt like leaving right away, but she decided to stay. The other girl might return, after all. However, even as the bright afternoon sunlight ripened into a deep, golden hue, Claudine didn\rquote t return.\par
By the time the maid who had brought Layla there showed up, it was already evening. \ldblquote You may go now,\rdblquote  she said in a much gentler tone than earlier. \ldblquote Miss Claudine said that you can keep the dress. And this, too.\rdblquote  She held out a glittering gold coin to her. \ldblquote Take it. It\rquote s proper manners to graciously accept things that your superiors give you. Got it?\rdblquote\par
