As the western sky started to turn a deep, rosy hue, Layla stepped out of the mansion. Walking out of the backdoor that connected to the rose\par
garden, she was greeted by a refreshing breeze.\par
Clutching the gold coin in her right hand, she walked with confident steps. However, her high spirits didn\rquote t last long. Claudine was there. She was sitting under the pergola, which was covered with climbing roses in full bloom, having a playful conversation with her cousins. As soon as she saw Layla, the same subtle smile from earlier returned to her face, and she said, \ldblquote Bye, Layla.\rdblquote\par
A few of the young men around her also looked over at Layla. Fortunately, Duke Herhardt was nowhere in sight. Layla bowed rather than saying anything in response. Claudine didn\rquote t criticize her for this, which suggested that it wasn\rquote t against propel\rquote  etiquette.\par
\par
Layla walked with short, quick steps until she was out of their sight, at which point she started running. She couldn\rquote t wait to leave this strange, unfamiliar world and return to Uncle Bill\rquote s cottage. However, just at the last moment, she suffered her worst misfortune of that day.\par
As she was crossing the stone-paved boundary between the garden and the forest path, she tripped and fell. The gold coin fell out of her hand and rolled on the pavement, then bumped against the tip of a man\rquote s shoe a few paces ahead. She gaped at the coin as it spun in place. The man casually lifted the front of his shoe and stepped on the coin, bringing it to rest.\par
Layla\rquote s eyes moved up from the man\rquote s well-polished shoes, to his long legs, finally reaching his face, which was looking down at her. It was Duke Herhardt.\par
Startled, Layla instinctively sprang to her feet. Her white dress was now stained with dirt and blood from her scraped knee. The duke looked at her calmly. A smile seemed to be breaking through on the edge of his lips. Keeping her own lips firmly shut, Layla brushed the dirt from her dress.\par
As she did this, Duke Herhardt took a leisurely step back. The coin that had been under his shoe gleamed, reflecting the light of the sunset. Layla had the urge to run away, but first she wanted to pick up the coin, so she approached the duke. As she was bending over to grab the coin, she suddenly remembered what Claudine had said as she was leaving.\par
\ldblquote She \rquote s no better than a puppy. \rdblquote\par
Those words had hurt her deeply. She took the coin and, holding it tightly in her hand, bowed politely to Duke Herhardt. Once she had started to bow, she didn\rquote t dare lift her head again. Holding her breath, she kept her head down as low as she could manage. Suddenly she realized that she no longer felt the pain she had felt all over her body from the fall. It was very strange.\par
\par
She started running again, leaving Duke Herhardt behind. Because her knee hurt, she couldn\rquote t run as fast as before, but she didn\rquote t stop. She could feel something welling up inside her. She only realized what it was once she had traversed the forest path and saw the light leaking out of the cottage.\par
It was sadness.\par
\par
