\ldblquote You can have this, Uncle,\rdblquote  Layla said, suppressing her sadness as she held out the gold coin. Bill\rquote s thick brows slowly winkled. \ldblquote What\rquote s that?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote A gold coin.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I know that. Where\rquote s you get it?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Miss Claudine gave it to me.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Claudine?\rdblquote  Ah yes, that young noble girl,\rdblquote  he replied, nodding.\par
For the two days since Layla had gone inside the mansion, she had been in low spirits. She hadn\rquote t felt like chatting, or even wandering the forest or the garden. Those two days had led Bill to realize how much he had grown accustomed to daily life with the little girl. Now that she had grown quiet, his life had grown quiet. And he didn\rquote t much like this quiet life.\par
Leaning towards the table where she was sitting, he asked, \ldblquote But why are you giving that money to me?\rdblquote\par
Straightening her posture, she looked straight in his face and replied, \ldblquote Because I think it\rquote s worth a lot.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You\rquote re right about that.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yeah, so I didn\rquote t think I should throw it away, even though receiving it made me feel bad. But I thought if I gave it to you, it could be a small way of\par
\par
repaying you for all you\rquote ve done for me.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Damn it,\rdblquote  he muttered impulsively. Layla flinched, but didn\rquote t mind.\par
The moment he had seen Layla running back from the mansion looking a mess, he had guessed that something had happened to her that had hurt her deep down inside. He knew what nobles were like. He hadn\rquote t asked her about it, because he had worried she would cry if he did. But now it was getting hard to hold back. \ldblquote Layla?\rdblquote\par
When she heard him say her name, the mature expression she had been maintaining gave way to a childlike look again.\par
\ldblquote You earned that money, so you should keep it.\rdblquote  \ldblquote I earned it?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote That\rquote s right. You earned it by working. You faced a noble girl who was bored to death. It\rquote s a lousy job, but you did great at it, so you should proudly accept your payment. It\rquote s only right.\rdblquote\par
Layla furrowed her brow, feeling perplexed. Bill gulped down his beer as he watched her sink into thought. After a few moments, she tilted her head and, tapping on the coin, asked, \ldblquote Really?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You bet,\rdblquote  he replied, wiping the foam from his beard with his sleeve.\par
I earned it. I earned it. As the thought echoed in her head repeatedly, her face brightened noticeably.\par
\ldblquote Welcome to the adult world, Layla,\rdblquote  Bill said, placing a large cut of meat on her plate.\par
\ldblquote Adult? Me?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote If someone can earn a living by their own labor, they\rquote re an adult. That\rquote s exactly what you did.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote But all I earned was one single gold coin\'85\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote There are people in this world several times your age who can barely manage that. You\rquote re off to a great start. I\rquote m sure you\rquote ll do just fine as an adult.\rdblquote  He filled the remaining space on her plate with bread and roasted vegetables.\par
Her eyes widened. \ldblquote Uncle, that\rquote s too much.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You\rquote ve been eating like a bird the last couple of days. You need to eat a lot tonight.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote But\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t you remember? I like kids who can eat like a cow.\rdblquote\par
She burst into laughter. \ldblquote Uncle, if I eat well, I\rquote ll grow a lot, right?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Sure. Why do you ask? Did someone call you short?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No, I just think I look too young. It bugs me.\rdblquote\par
That\rquote s because you are young, Bill almost replied, before stopping himself.\par
Layla started slicing her meat confidently.\par
Looking at her, Bill realized that she had grown quite a lot in the few months she had been there. She was no longer as thin as a rail. She looked nice. Her natural frame was small and slender like that of a bird, so she wasn\rquote t likely to ever grow to be big and tall. Still, he had no doubt that she would become a beautiful woman one day. This thought suddenly made him uneasy.\par
For poverty-stricken women, good looks were a dangerous thing, and could easily get them into trouble. He knew he had to send her somewhere he could trust, but could he trust an orphanage? Orphanages seemed like the perfect place to send a child if you wanted to ruin their life.\par
Damn this world and all the awful people in it, he thought. He then finished his beer while internally cursing all the people who had abandoned the girl and left her to him.\par
He thought back on his earlier life, in which all he had cared about was making sure that the flowers bloomed beautifully and the fruits ripened\par
\par
deliciously. How had his life come to be full of such worry and anxiety?\par
\ldblquote Uncle, if it\rquote s my money, I can spend it, right?\rdblquote  Layla asked in between bites of food.\par
\ldblquote Of course. Is there something you want?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yeah. I filled up my notebook. I want to buy a new one.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Then go ahead.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Do you think I could buy some colored pencils too?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Absolutely.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Is there anything you need, Uncle?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Why? Do you want to buy me something?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Yes.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote What if I asked you to get me something really expensive?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Then I\rquote d save up a bunch of money and buy it for you,\rdblquote  she replied with a serious look on her face. When she got serious, her eyes seemed to shine with a darker shade of green, which made her look cutely intelligent.\par
Bill laughed loudly, then poured himself another drink. He then set down a glass of apple juice in front of Layla. She lifted the glass and gestured for him to clink his against hers. He happily obliged. Smiling brightly, she then downed her juice in one gulp. Suddenly, he grew worried that doing this could lead to her becoming a drunkard. Soon, though, he shook his head, thinking, She\rquote s only here temporarily. He then emptied his glass.\par
