Matthias had the car stop at the end of the mansion\rquote s driveway, far from the mansion. The chauffeur and the butler looked flustered.\par
Duke Herhardt\rquote s arrival had been sudden. After news had hit that he would be arriving a week earlier than originally scheduled, the Arvis staff had been\par
\par
busily working from dawn to dusk, preparing for his arrival. As a result, only two people had been sent out to pick him up. And now the duke had sprung this surprise on them.\par
Hessen, the butler, gulped nervously and said, \ldblquote Master, there\rquote s still-\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m going to walk around for a bit,\rdblquote  Matthias calmly said, cutting Hessen off. The chauffeur wavered for a moment, then rushed to get out of the car and open the rear passenger door.\par
\ldblquote No,\rdblquote  Matthias said, shaking his head at Hessen, who had started to follow him out of the car. \ldblquote I\rquote ll see you in the mansion.\rdblquote  He smiled at Hessen and then turned and started walking away.\par
Hessen hesitated uncertainly for a moment, then followed his master\rquote s order and got back into the car. The chauffeur also hurried back into the driver\rquote s seat. After they drove off, the road was quiet again. Clutching his officer\rquote s cap loosely in one hand, Matthias sauntered around under the shade of the trees. The click of his boots blended pleasingly with sound of the leaves swaying in the breeze.\par
Matthias von Herhardt had been the perfect child, the perfect student, and had become the perfect officer. Now he would soon be marrying the perfect woman and would then become the perfect husband and the perfect father. This incessant series of perfections had come so naturally, that the whole process had started to bore him.\par
He slowed his pace a bit. A beam of light streaming through a gap in the leaves fell on his eyes, accentuating the slight redness at their edge. As he walked a little further, the beam descended and illuminated his golden belt buckle and the badges on the breast of his slate-gray uniform.\par
\ldblquote You need to get engaged this summer.\rdblquote\par
\par
Matthias had readily gone along with his mother\rquote s wishes. It was his duty to get married at the proper time and father an heir.\par
\ldblquote I believe that Claudine is the most suitable candidate to be the next duchess.\rdblquote\par
He had also accepted his grandmother\rquote s opinion with good grace. Claudine von Brandt was a well-qualified prospective bride with an excellent pedigree. Matthias had never desired anything. He hadn\rquote t had the opportunity\emdash before he ever had the chance to desire something, it had already been given to him. Desire itself was an alien concept to him. So was marriage.\par
He aimed for a perfect marriage. A marriage that served as a steppingstone to stabilize his world, without involving needless emotions. Claudine von Brandt seemed like the best fit for that kind of arrangement, and that was enough for him. He wasn\rquote t particularly interested in her in any other respect. He didn\rquote t feel the need to be.\par
He stopped walking for a moment in the middle of the road. He looked up, and rays of broken sunlight dazzled his eyes. It was then that he sensed the presence of another person nearby.\par
He looked around with narrowed eyes, and soon saw a young lady riding towards him on a bicycle. He remained standing in place, and soon she passed by him on his left. Her golden hair rippled in the breeze.\par
Layla Llewellyn?\par
As the name popped into his mind, the young lady turned her head towards him. When she saw him, her green eyes widened. As she continued to ride while locking eyes with him, she lost her balance and the bicycle toppled over. The sound of her screams followed immediately. The front wheel of the upside-down bicycle continued to spin.\par
Matthias walked up to the young lady on the ground. Under his shadow, she lifted her head. There was no mistaking it, she was Layla Llewellyn, the\par
\par
little girl who was crazy about birds.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m sorry, Your Grace,\rdblquote  she quickly said, bowing her head. She seemed to be waiting for him to go on his way. Matthias was about to leave when he changed his mind and casually looked down at Layla. Her school uniform was covered with dirt. Her stockings were ripped, and her knees were bleeding. Silence descended as the bicycle wheel slowed to a stop.\par
Layla looked up at Matthias with squinted eyes. She had her usual, plucky expression, and yet there was something gentle in her look.\par
Looks like that girl has grown up as well, he thought. While it was natural that she would have grown along with the passage of time, seeing her was giving him a strange feeling. In his mind, she had always remained a little girl. A little girl who had gotten on his nerves by always trying to avoid him. But the Layla Llewellyn he saw before him now didn\rquote t match the little girl he remembered at all.\par
The soft curve of her body underneath the thin fabric of her summer school uniform was nothing like that of the scrawny little girl. Nor were her bright lips and cheeks, or the soft scent being wafted by the breeze from her body.\par
This realization gave Matthias a strange sense of uneasiness as Layla struggled to get up. She took a step backward to fix her shoes, then brushed the dust off her uniform. Although she was now fully grown, she was still quite short, barely reaching his chin when she stood up straight.\par
\ldblquote Layla Llewellyn,\rdblquote  Matthias said impulsively.\par
Layla flinched and hunched her shoulders. Her head was still bowed. \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry, Your Grace,\rdblquote  she said, then repeated the apology several more times as she crouched down to pick up her belongings, which were strewn about his feet\emdash her bag, her books, her notebook.\par
\par
Matthias\rquote s gaze followed her hands, which were soiled with dirt and blood until her finger brushed against a pen. He took a step forward and leisurely placed his foot on top of the pen. At this, Layla finally looked up at him. She looked irritated.\par
\ldblquote Layla Llewellyn,\rdblquote  he said once again. \ldblquote I\rquote m talking to you.\rdblquote  He was speaking in a relaxed manner, just like the relaxed way he had stepped on the pen.\par
She shut her eyes tightly and opened them again. \ldblquote Yes, Your Grace?\rdblquote  She looked like she was waiting for the chance to snatch the pen back, but Matthias didn\rquote t move.\par
\ldblquote Go ahead,\rdblquote  she said. \ldblquote I\rquote m listening.\rdblquote  Her voice was full of confidence even though her body was trembling. Her green eyes, which resembled the summer forest, twinkled with anger and embarrassment.\par
The memory of him smiling as he stepped on the gold coin, all those years ago, flashed into her mind. He had been looking at her with the same expression that day as he was now. Finally, he laughed quietly to himself and casually walked past her. He placed his cap on his head and started strolling down the road again as if nothing had happened.\par
She stared at his back as he walked away. Why was he acting that way if he didn\rquote t actually have anything to say? she wondered, squeezing the pen in her hand. Would the residents of Arvis believe that Duke Herhardt, the man they praise as the perfect nobleman, would do something like that? I\rquote d bet all the money I\rquote ve saved up for eyeglasses that they wouldn\rquote t. They\rquote d call me crazy.\par
With her lips shut tight, she quickly set her bicycle upright again, then thoroughly wiped off the pen and placed it in her bag. She started walking her bicycle down the road, following behind Duke Herhardt, who was walking quite slowly. Her skinned knees stung. Even though she was sure that he\par
\par
wouldn\rquote t look back, she maintained a good posture as she walked. It hurt a lot to walk without limping, but she endured the pain.\par
I sure wish he\rquote d put those long legs of his to good use and pick up the pace.\par
She was about to let out an irritated sigh when the duke turned around. A gentle breeze was blowing through the leaves, causing the broken sunbeams to slowly undulate on the summer road.\par
Startled, Layla froze, forgetting even to avert her eyes. Matthias\rquote s gaze slowly moved down her long, loose hair. It passed by her chest, which was gently rising and falling, and her white hands, which were gripping the handlebars of her bicycle. It came to rest on her amazingly slender ankles and her tiny feet.\par
It then returned to her eyes. He gazed tranquilly at those clear, green eyes for a long while. Layla Llewellyn was still an orphan living for free on his estate, but one key fact had changed everything. As time passed, the little girl had grown up. As this fact dawned on him, he finally saw Layla for who she was now.\par
She was no longer a little girl. She was now a young lady.\par
