On the surface, Claudine von Brandt\rquote s visit to Arvis was treated as a standard early-summer visit to a relative\rquote s estate.\par
However, everyone knew that there was another reason for her visit.\par
The two families were meeting to negotiate details and grow closer before the engagement was publicly announced. This goal was clear to all, and no one tried to conceal it, least of all Claudine herself.\par
\ldblquote Hello, Duke Herhardt,\rdblquote  Claudine said, curtsying to Matthias with perfect etiquette.\par
Matthias saw no trace of the young, immature cousin he remembered from years earlier. \ldblquote Welcome, Lady Claudine,\rdblquote  he replied, bowing in response to her curtsy. It was as if he were greeting a lady he had just met for the first time. They exchanged graceful smiles.\par
\par
There was nothing much new or surprising for them to discuss. They had never been particularly close, but they knew each other fairly well after seeing each other countless times over the years. They knew that each of them, Matthias von Herhardt and Claudine von Brandt both, were nobles to the core. This was the biggest and most straightforward reason why they had chosen each other.\par
Matthias deftly escorted Claudine to the greenhouse attached to the back side of the mansion. His mother, Elise von Herhardt, had asked the servants to prepare their afternoon tea in the greenhouse, because she knew how much Claudine liked it.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m always struck by the beauty of this greenhouse. It\rquote s as if Lady Herhardt borrowed a bit of heaven and is storing it in here,\rdblquote  Claudine joyfully remarked after taking a sip of tea and setting her cup down without a sound. She was speaking in the cheerful yet restrained manner expected of a young noblewoman.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote ve put my heart and soul into selecting and arranging everything in here. I\rquote m looking forward to passing it on to a lady of the house who will appreciate its value,\rdblquote  Elise softly replied.\par
Hearing this, Countess Brandt looked proudly at her daughter. Claudine smiled modestly, saying nothing in response.\par
\ldblquote Matthias, you should give Claudine a tour of Arvis\rquote s heaven,\rdblquote  Elise whispered to her son as the teatime was drawing to a close.\par
It seemed that it was time for negotiations to begin between the families. Matthias extended his hand to Claudine, who then lightly placed her hand in his. Gazing down at the young lady\rquote s hand, which was inside an elegant white- glove made of translucent lace, Matthias was briefly reminded of Layla\rquote s small hands, stained with dirt and blood.\par
\par
They conversed on suitable topics as they strolled along. A gentle jet of water babbled in the marble fountain that was the centerpiece of the greenhouse. Meanwhile, the clear sound of birdsong brightened the languid afternoon mood.\par
Claudine observed Matthias out of the corner of her eye. Despite the gentle smile on his face, it was impossible to discern his inner feelings, or his attitude about the world. He had flawless manners, but at his core, he was a man full of arrogance that came from never having had to bow down or submit to anyone in his life. Claudine liked this aspect of him.\par
\ldblquote The birds in here are so pretty,\rdblquote  she commented, admiring the many colorful birds perched on tree branches in the greenhouse. Matthias hadn\rquote t even noticed that there were birds in the greenhouse until Claudine had pointed them out.\par
His mother, who was still Arvis\rquote s lady of the house, loved birds every bit as much as she loved roses. Just as she kept a gardener on staff to tend the roses, she had hired a bird-keeper to look after the birds. Her own role, as she saw it, was to enjoy the products of their work. That was her outlook on life in general\emdash to her the world was a lovely place full of things prepared by others for her enjoyment.\par
\ldblquote This one is so obedient. It\rquote s remarkable. I wonder what the secret is,\rdblquote  Claudine exclaimed, smiling at the small bird that had just landed on her extended hand.\par
Matthias glanced around the greenhouse and realized that the birds in it were incredibly docile. The windows were wide open, and yet none of them were flying away. They were content to remain in the peaceful world of the greenhouse, singing beautiful melodies.\par
\par
He watched a parrot shuffle along its perch, then looked at the finch warbling as it sat on Claudine\rquote s hand. He turned and made a gesture to the bird-keeper who was politely standing in wait nearby. The gray-haired man quietly approached and stood beside Claudine, then explained, \ldblquote I clip the birds\rquote  wings, Miss. That makes it so that they can\rquote t fly very far, so that they won\rquote t escape. It also makes them more obedient. Birds capable of flying freely are almost impossible to tame.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You clip their wings?\rdblquote  she asked. \ldblquote Doesn\rquote t that hurt them?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No, it\rquote s just their feathers, so it doesn\rquote t hurt. It\rquote s good for them, in fact, because it keeps them from flying away and getting lost or injured. If you\rquote re interested, I could show you how I do it.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Would that be all right, Duke Herhardt?\rdblquote  she asked excitedly. \ldblquote As you wish, My Lady,\rdblquote  Matthias readily replied.\par
The bird-keeper then led them to a very large birdcage at one end of the greenhouse. It contained young birds that hadn\rquote t yet had their wings clipped. He reached in and retrieved a bright-yellow bird, the most beautiful bird of the bunch, and brought it to his worktable.\par
\ldblquote What kind of bird is that?\rdblquote  Matthias asked.\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s a canary, Your Grace. It\rquote s a songbird that sings beautifully.\rdblquote  The bird- keeper proceeded to place a small handkerchief over the bird\rquote s head, and used one hand to gently spread apart the feathers of one of its wings. With his other hand, he picked up a small pair of scissors and immediately made a few quick clips. The feathers fluttered down onto his worktable. He did the same to the other wing and then set the bird down.\par
The canary flapped its wings desperately, but was only able to fly a short distance before flopping down on the ground. Still not accepting that it could no longer fly, it tried a few more times, with the same result.\par
\par
Matthias reached down and picked up the bird from the edge of the flowerbed. The bird squirmed in his hand and started to cry, making sounds closer to shrieks than to song.\par
\ldblquote They don\rquote t become tame right away, Your Grace. It takes time for them to get used to having clipped wings,\rdblquote  the bird-keeper said as Matthias handed him back the canary, which he then gently stroked to calm it down.\par
\ldblquote Would you like to try doing it yourself, Miss?\rdblquote  the bird-keeper asked Claudine.\par
\ldblquote No, just seeing it is enough for me. Thank you for satisfying my curiosity.\rdblquote  She then turned away. \ldblquote Let\rquote s go back to the tea table,\rdblquote  she said, extending her hand to Matthias. Once again, as Matthias looked at her smooth, beautiful hand, his mind flashed back momentarily to the image of Layla\rquote s dirty, bloody hands.\par
Before they walked away from the bird keeper, Matthias gave him an impulsive command. \ldblquote Bring that to my bedroom.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Pardon\'85?\rdblquote  the bird-keeper asked, looking surprised.\par
\ldblquote That bird,\rdblquote  Matthias replied, glancing with narrowed eyes at the bird. \ldblquote My canary.\par
