8.\par
A Safe World.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
The doorman looked very flustered.\par
Layla nodded to him, as if she understood that his surprise was due to the fact that this hotel, the finest hotel in the city, was not the type of place where people with ratty old bicycles would normally come.\par
The doorman looked warily at Layla, then glanced at Duke Herhardt and Claudine, then back at Layla a few times. Finally, he reluctantly let Layla enter and had a bellhop take her bicycle to storage. Layla thanked him and walked into the hotel.\par
The tea lounge, which included an outdoor patio area, was located on the main lobby\rquote s right side. The manager hurried over to greet them, then led them to a table on the patio, facing the river. Layla was the last one seated. Much to her surprise, she was placed directly across from Duke Herhardt.\par
\par
She was immediately reminded of the way he had stared at her that day at the river, so she quickly lowered her gaze. The thin gold frame of her eyeglasses glittered in the summer sun streaming in obliquely under the awning. The duke was looking at her casually, as if there was no reason for awkwardness between them.\par
Why do I feel so embarrassed when he was the one who was seen naked, not me? Layla wondered. I suppose it\rquote s because nobles see commoners like me as more like furniture or paintings than as real people. No one would feel embarrassed to be naked in front of a piece of furniture, or worry that a painting would be shocked to see them nude.\par
As Layla came to this conclusion, the tea table was set. A cup of strong coffee was placed in front of Duke Herhardt, without him having to request it. He lifted the cup with his long, smooth fingers and took a sip.\par
He and Claudine then began conversing, seemingly having forgotten entirely about Layla. They discussed various topics, including the art exhibit they had visited earlier that day, news about their relatives, and the party that was planned for the upcoming weekend. Matthias\rquote s soft, deep voice gave way to Claudine\rquote s clear, high voice at regular intervals.\par
Layla didn\rquote t understand why they had asked her to come along if they weren\rquote t interested in talking to her, but she decided not to think about it. Claudine\rquote s behavior was beyond her understanding in many ways. This had been true ever since the summer when they had first met, when Layla was twelve.\par
\ldblquote So, Layla, how is school? Is it fun?\rdblquote  Claudine asked in an even higher voice as she set her teacup down on its saucer. Even though she was only a year older than Layla, she always talked to her the way an adult would talk to a small child.\par
\par
\ldblquote Yes, Miss,\rdblquote  Layla replied. In her mind, she was repeating the phrase For Uncle Bill over and over again, as she had grown accustomed to doing to lend herself patience around Claudine.\par
Claudine nodded contently, then asked a few more questions about school life. Layla responded to each question by smiling politely and saying, \ldblquote Yes, Miss.\rdblquote  She knew that this was exactly what Claudine expected from her.\par
\ldblquote I suppose you\rquote ll be graduating next year, then?\rdblquote  Claudine asked, still speaking in a friendly tone despite the look of boredom on her face. \ldblquote Yes, Miss,\rdblquote  Layla replied, continuing to play the part that had been assigned to her.\par
\ldblquote What are you planning to do after graduating?\rdblquote  \ldblquote I\rquote m going to get certified as a teacher.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote A teacher\'85?\rdblquote  Claudine asked, drawing out the phrase as she lifted her teacup. As she nodded, the ribbon and corsage on her small hat bobbed up and down. \ldblquote That\rquote s excellent, Layla. It\rquote s a wonderful goal. I think that will suit you well.\rdblquote  She sounded like she was praising a child. \ldblquote Don\rquote t you think so, Duke Herhardt?\rdblquote\par
Without thinking about it, Layla followed Claudine\rquote s gaze and turned towards Duke Herhardt. Looking at him with her glasses on, the clear blue of his eyes stood out strikingly, so much so that it took a moment for it to register with her that those eyes were looking right at her, whereupon she instantly lowered her gaze again.\par
\ldblquote I suppose so,\rdblquote  he casually agreed.\par
After that polite display of interest in Layla, they seemed to forget about her again. This brought her a sense of relief. She hoped that this tedious teatime would be over soon. She was supposed to meet Kyle downtown after his tennis practice. She hoped this wouldn\rquote t cause her to be late.\par
\par
Feeling antsy, she looked up. As she did so, Matthias turned to look at her.\par
Rather than averting her eyes this time, she stared right back at him.\par
When she was young, his eyes had reminded her of blue marbles, and she had once imagined them knocking against each other and making a crisp clinking noise. As this childish thought flashed back into her mind, she felt even more distant from the duke. It was as if the newfound clarity in her eyesight had increased the clarity of her feelings as well. Her lip started to twitch as her discomfort grew. Soon she found herself saying, \ldblquote I beg your pardon, Your Grace and Miss Claudine, but do you mind if I excuse myself?\rdblquote  She turned her gaze from Matthias to Claudine, which allowed her to steady her breathing somewhat. \ldblquote I\rquote m supposed to meet a friend soon,\rdblquote  she added with a tinge of anxiousness in her voice.\par
Claudine let out a soft chuckle, then granted her permission by nodding. Layla breathed a sigh of relief. She then politely bid farewell and hurried out of the hotel. After the bellhop retrieved her bicycle, she jumped on and set off down the busy city street. Strangely, the farther away she rode from the duke, the clearer his face became in her mind.\par
It must be because of the glasses, she thought as she panted from the exertion. I saw him more clearly, so I\rquote m remembering him more clearly. This rationalization made her feel better. What would have happened if I\rquote d had my glasses on that fateful day at the riverside?\par
Just as her thoughts were growing troubled again, she arrived at the meeting place. Kyle, who had already arrived, waved eagerly at her, smiling brightly. She was back in the comfortable, safe world she was used to.\par
