\ldblquote Is that really her? The orphan that the gardener was taking care of?\rdblquote\par
Countess Brandt asked, furrowing her brow as she gazed out the window at the garden where a bespectacled young lady was helping the gardener tend the rose bed.\par
\ldblquote Yes, Mother. It\rquote s Layla, that girl,\rdblquote  Claudine casually replied as she concentrated on her needlework. She was just as skilled at embroidery as ever. While her mother paced around by the window, she patiently made stitch after stitch. As she did so, roses of various colors seemed to bloom from her fingers. \ldblquote She\rquote s a pretty young lady. Don\rquote t you think so, Mother? I think she\rquote ll only get prettier as she continues to mature.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote And that doesn\rquote t bother you?\rdblquote  Countess Brandt asked.\par
\ldblquote I understand your concern, Mother,\rdblquote  Claudine replied as she set down her embroidery frame with a content smile.\par
\par
Countess Brandt\rquote s eyes were wide with worry. She had always had a delicate frame, and had suffered several miscarriages before finally giving birth to a healthy child. That child was the Brandt family\rquote s only child, Claudine.\par
Countess Brandt had thus lived in constant fear that her husband would leave her for her failure to provide him with a male heir. Her husband\rquote s mistress had also failed to bear him a son, but this provided little consolation to Countess Brandt. She always worried that a pretty young lady would show up one day, bear him a son, and steal away his love.\par
Claudine understood this about her mother, and while she did feel sorry for her, she was also irritated by her constant fretting. \ldblquote But I don\rquote t like to dwell on things like that, Mother,\rdblquote  she solemnly declared.\par
The countess sighed in exasperation.\par
\ldblquote You\rquote re still too young to understand men, Claudine. If I were you\'85\rdblquote  \ldblquote You\rquote d get rid of all the pretty ladies in the world so that Duke Herhardt\par
wouldn\rquote t be tempted by them, right?\rdblquote  Claudine asked, giggling as she let out an exaggerated sigh. \ldblquote As you say, I\rquote m still young and don\rquote t know much about men yet. But even I know that men who hold a high position in society will typically have a mistress or two.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Goodness, Claudine!\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Naturally, I would prefer if he didn\rquote t take a mistress, but if he does, it won\rquote t exactly shock me,\rdblquote  Claudine said, shrugging.\par
Despite her self-possessed exterior, Claudine had been taken aback when she and Matthias had run into Layla. Layla had been a pretty young girl, so it was only natural that she would develop into a pretty young lady. But Claudine had been shocked to see just how beautiful she had become.\par
She had the distinctive, nymph-like petite frame and dainty features typical of ladies from Lovita. Her mysterious green eyes and flawlessly smooth\par
\par
skin only added to this impression.\par
Claudine had decided to invite her along to tea for exactly this reason. She was curious how the duke would react to such a lady. And his reaction had been just as she had expected. He had shown a proper balance between interest and indifference, while maintaining perfect dignity and self control. For Claudine, that had been enough.\par
\ldblquote But Claudine, nothing good can come from letting a young lady such as her be around Matthias.\rdblquote  Countess Brandt still looked worried. \ldblquote Perhaps I\rquote ll speak with his mother or his grandmother. What do you think?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Mother\'85\rdblquote  Claudine grumbled. It bothered her the way her mother was always crying over matters of love even though she had every luxury and comfort in the world at her fingertips. She had firmly resolved not to follow in her mother\rquote s footsteps in that respect.\par
That was one of the reasons that she had agreed to get engaged to Matthias. She knew that it was naive to expect a man to only ever have feelings for a wife that he had obtained through an arranged marriage. Only people who believed in the happy endings of fairy tales would be foolish enough to believe such a thing.\par
For all she knew, Matthias might end up getting swept away by lust for beautiful women, just like her father had. Just like countless men did. As she saw it, there was no real danger in a husband having a mistress, as long as he treated her like a mistress. It was men who treated their mistresses like wives that were problematic. Everything Claudine knew about Matthias suggested that he was in the former category.\par
Do I love him ? she asked herself, smiling. Well, maybe\'85 maybe not. It doesn\rquote t really matter either way. And he is well aware of this too.\par
\par
What really mattered to her was whether Duke Herhardt would fulfill his role as a husband and father with the same faithfulness and devotion that he showed in fulfilling all his other duties. That was what Claudine wanted. A marriage that would allow her to preserve her dignity.\par
\ldblquote Is it such a big deal if Duke Herhardt is interested in that lowly, beautiful girl?\rdblquote  she calmly asked her mother.\par
Countess Brandt gasped. \ldblquote My goodness, Claudine! Listen to yourself!\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote At worst, she\rquote ll end up as his mistress,\rdblquote  Claudine said, chuckling quietly. \ldblquote As mistresses go, she wouldn\rquote t be that bad. She poses no real threat to me. I have her tamed like a puppy.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Oh, Claudine. You really\'85 know nothing about love,\rdblquote  Countess Brandt lamented.\par
Gazing into her mother\rquote s eyes, which were always wet with tears precisely because of love, Claudine smiled faintly, then turned her head towards the window. Outside, she could see Layla, clutching her waist as she laughed about something.\par
