Just as Layla was about to leave the cabin, she heard Matthias\rquote s voice. After a few seconds, she realized that his words had been directed at her. Holding her breath, she turned to face him again.\par
Matthias was sitting across from his butler at a table by the window. He was reviewing documents that were piled on the table, as he had been doing for some time. Without looking up, he said, \ldblquote The ones you brought are too\par
\par
gaudy. Go get some with more muted colors.\rdblquote  He then looked up at Layla with an extremely gentle smile on his face.\par
Layla clenched her fists to keep her irritation from boiling over and turning into rage. It seemed that there was a problem with the roses she had selected. The roses of which he had simply asked her to bring \ldblquote an assortment,\rdblquote  without further specification.\par
While she stared at the roses she had brought, Matthias turned his attention back to his work. His butler Hessen continued his report, while Matthias listened closely, interjecting with the occasional brief instruction. They were behaving as though their errand girl were no longer there.\par
Feeling frustrated, she repeated the phrase for Uncle Bill in her mind as she quietly left the cabin. He makes people jump through hoops for him at two in the afternoon in the middle of summer, and people call him a perfect gentleman? she grumbled as she trudged back to the garden.\par
For the most part, Layla had little interest in the lives of those who lived in the mansion, but she was aware that the cabin by the river was Duke Herhardt\rquote s private getaway spot. He rarely had guests there, and only a limited number of servants would wait on him.\par
She figured that he had wanted the flowers because of Claudine. Since they would soon be getting engaged, she had thought that perhaps Claudine had started joining him at the cabin occasionally. That was why she had chosen the most colorful roses she could find. She knew that Claudine liked flashy colors. But it had turned out that this was a mistake.\par
She made it through the woods, and stepped boldly out into the bright, shadowless garden, where she carefully cut a new selection of roses. To meet the duke\rquote s fastidious demand, she chose only soft, muted colors this time. They happened to color she preferred as well, for the most part.\par
\par
With her basket full of roses in hand, she started walking again in the oppressive heat of the sunny summer afternoon.\par
If he was going to be so picky, why didn\rquote t he tell me what colors he wanted in the first place? she thought, venting her anger by kicking stones from the path as she walked. I hate the duke. As her irritation continued to build, she started kicking innocent leaves as well.\par
By the time she arrived back at the cabin, she was starting to feel dizzy from the heat.\par
The cabin, which had originally been an old boathouse by the dock, had been renovated into a beautiful building that looked like it was half-floating on the river. The first floor housed the boat storage area from the original boathouse, along with a small kitchen where simple refreshments could be prepared. The second floor was the duke\rquote s private space and contained a drawing-room, a bedroom, and a dining room.\par
Holding the basket full of roses, Layla climbed the outdoor staircase up to the second floor. The butler and a maid were just then leaving. She greeted them and then hurried into the drawing-room. Matthias was still in the same spot, sitting with his eyes closed and his head leaning backward.\par
Should I wait for him to wake up? she wondered. Luckily, he soon opened his eyes. His slightly messy hair was covering his forehead.\par
\ldblquote I brought the new roses, Your Grace,\rdblquote  she said, holding up the basket. Matthias just stared at her, saying nothing. With his riding jacket removed and the top few buttons of his shirt undone, he looked much more vulnerable and languorous than before.\par
\ldblquote Should I\'85 go get different ones again?\rdblquote  she cautiously asked in a quivering voice. If he makes me go back to the garden for the third time, I\rquote m going to throw the roses at him.\par
\par
\ldblquote If I said yes, would you go?\rdblquote  he asked in a slightly drowsy voice.\par
\ldblquote If I made another mistake, then of course I\rquote ll have to go. But in that case, please tell me exactly what colors you would like,\rdblquote  she replied. She had intended to respond with a simple Yes, Your Grace, but the response that came out of her mouth was much longer. She froze, afraid that her response had been impolite.\par
Matthias stared at her for a moment, then straightened his posture in his seat and said, \ldblquote Sit down,\rdblquote  gesturing at the spot across the table from him.\par
\ldblquote No, Your Grace. If you are satisfied with these colors, then I should be\'85\rdblquote  \ldblquote Since you brought the flowers, isn\rquote t it your duty to put them in a vase,\par
Lady Layla?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote But Your Grace, I am not skilled in flower arrangement.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Do you expect me to do it, then?\rdblquote  he asked, glancing around the room and then back at her. She could easily understand the meaning of that simple gesture, he was indicating that there was no one else in the cabin but them. That is, he was telling her that she needed to put the flowers in a vase, no matter how unskilled she was at flower arrangement.\par
She cautiously approached him. She didn\rquote t have the courage to sit in the seat across from him, so she sat on a chair by the window that faced the river.\par
Once she had started trimming the roses so that they could be placed in a vase, he turned back to the pile of documents on the table. The silent room was soon filled with the sound of clippers snipping rose stems, and papers being turned and shifted.\par
As he signed the final document, he suddenly remembered the bird in his bedroom back at the mansion. Despite the bird keeper\rquote s warning that canaries were hard to tame, this canary had proved very obedient. It would readily sit on Matthias\rquote s finger and sing.\par
\par
He often found himself staring at the tiny bird, amazed at how beautifully it sang. Sometimes it would flap its wings and fly a short distance, then return to his finger and start singing again, as if it were talking to a friend.\par
While he sorted the documents, Layla quietly walked to the bathroom to fill a vase with water. She moved smoothly, as if she were weightless.\par
When she returned, he could tell that she hadn\rquote t been lying about her lack of flower arrangement skills. The roses looked like they had been recklessly crammed into the vase.\par
\ldblquote Do you like it, Your Grace?\rdblquote  she asked hesitantly.\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s dreadful,\rdblquote  he replied in a dry tone that didn\rquote t sound upset or critical. She blinked, taken aback, and her cheeks started to redden. \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry,\par
Your Grace. I will go fetch a maid who is skilled in this sort of thing.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Sit.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Pardon?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Sit down, Layla,\rdblquote  he said with a quieter voice that only heightened the pressure she felt. His finger was pointing directly at the seat across the table from his. She placed the shameful rose vase on a credenza and stiffly sat where he was pointing.\par
\ldblquote Eat,\rdblquote  he said, now looking at a tray that was covered by a dome-shaped silver cloche.\par
She awkwardly lifted the cloche off the tray, and discovered a sandwich and a glass of lemonade.\par
He quietly watched her as she reacted with puzzlement. Knowing that she liked sandwiches, he had intended the snack as payment for her work. Even though the product of her labors had been awful, the fact remained that she had performed a service for him in the gardener\rquote s stead.\par
\par
\ldblquote Thank you, Your Grace, but I\rquote m all right,\rdblquote  she said, placing the cloche back on the tray with a trembling hand. Her face, which had been so brightly smiling when she was with the doctor\rquote s son, was now full of troubled perplexity. \ldblquote If there\rquote s nothing else you need, I should be on my\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Layla.\rdblquote  He was now practically whispering. She stared at him, blinking slowly.\par
He took a sip of the whiskey soda in front of him. Beads of condensation from the crystal glass flowed slowly down his fingers. \ldblquote Did you think it was a request?\rdblquote  His moistened lips made a smile.\par
