\ldblquote Your Grace\'85?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Have you got it? If you find the answer, maybe I\rquote ll start practicing the friendly hunting you requested.\rdblquote  He then walked away, leaving Layla behind in a daze.\par
He didn\rquote t want anything in particular from Layla. He just wanted her to stay in her place. As the orphan living in his forest. As the inquisitive schoolgirl. And soon, as a teacher. Those roles constituted her proper place.\par
He mounted his horse, then looked back toward the thicket of bushes. Layla was crouched down in front of the dead bird. Her cheeks were glistening, presumably from tears. This gave him a feeling of contentment.\par
He had been born into a world of perfect order, and he had become its master. Within that order, everything was simple and clear. He didn\rquote t find it difficult at all to live in accordance with the expectations of his role. Sometimes it was so easy as to be boring. To his grandmother and mother, he was a worthy heir. To his staff, he was a generous master. On the battlefield, he was a noble soldier. To the directors of his company, he was a capable businessman.\par
\par
In all relationships in his life, there was a proper role for him to play, and he played all these roles willingly. The same was true of the corresponding parties on the other side of each such relationship. They played their proper roles, with the appropriate attitudes and feelings dictated by the world\rquote s order. These were the feelings that Matthias had seen, heard, and learned.\par
But what about that poor orphan who lives in my forest? He narrowed his eyes as he looked at Layla. She is nothing.\par
He smiled at the ease with which he had reached this conclusion.\par
It was the first time that he had encountered something that was nothing. There had never been room in his life for an entity without a pre-ordained role.\par
Now that there was such an entity, he felt strange, but not in a bad way. In fact, the emotions that that woman who was nothing was displaying were bringing him joy, even though they were outside the normal order of things in his world. It was the same joy that he felt when he killed a flying bird in one shot.\par
Her tears were best of all. He liked seeing Layla cry. She was pretty when she cried. Pretty enough that he wanted to make her cry again and again.\par
He left the forest feeling refreshed.\par
He then returned to the mansion and repeated the same routine he had been through countless times before. A lively dinner. Exquisitely vapid conversation. Cold champagne and bubbly laughter.\par
