Matthias returned to the mansion and found Kyle Ettman still standing next to Mrs. Ettman. The Arndts seemed very interested in marrying off their daughter to the Ettman family, and Mrs. Ettman seemed just as enthusiastic. Kyle, on the other hand, was visibly uncomfortable, but his mother didn\rquote t seem to care about how he felt.\par
Matthias became interested again in this tedious matchmaking encounter. Among the familiar faces at the party, he resumed his role as Duke Herhardt, lord of Arvis.\par
Layla had cried. Thinking back on that moment, he felt a deep sense of satisfaction. Unable to contain her indignation, her whole body had started trembling, and then thick tears had streamed down her face. Her usually clear eyes had appeared even clearer in that moment. Moistened by the tears, they had resembled the emeralds on the necklace she was wearing. This had pleased Matthias.\par
Once she cried for him like this, he had gladly returned her shoes. On his way out of the garden, he had looked back and seen her standing in the same place, still crying. He had begun walking with lighter steps at the thought that, since he was the reason she was crying, her tears were his.\par
Those tears had washed away the displeasure he had felt went he had first seen Kyle Ettman escorting her into the mansion. They had also washed away\par
\par
the strange, indescribable feeling that had come over him when he had kissed Claudine while looking at Layla.\par
That night, among all his possessions, Layla was the most beautiful to him, and the beautiful Layla had cried because of him. For this reason, it was a perfect summer night.\par
Just as he was reaching this satisfying conclusion, Layla suddenly appeared again. She was standing on the walkway between the terrace and the hall, looking for someone.\par
\ldblquote Kyle Ettman,\rdblquote  Matthias whispered to himself.\par
Layla seemed to have spotted Kyle. But she wasn\rquote t approaching him. She seemed to have figured out the significance of the people gathered around him, in particular Baron Arndt\rquote s daughter.\par
