The servant\rquote s eyes widened at this command. He glanced uncertainly in Kyle\rquote s direction, but soon responded as Matthias knew he would: \ldblquote Yes, Your Grace.\rdblquote  He then turned around and hurriedly walked into the crowd to fulfill the order he had been given.\par
Matthias went out onto the terrace and leaned against the column where Layla had just been. Layla was already far away, walking along the path that went through the garden. Her feet seemed to hurt a lot, because she was limping, and soon she removed her shoes and started walking barefoot.\par
\par
Matthias watched her as he waited for the servant to return. He knew that the servant would fail to find Count Klein. The count had greeted him earlier in the evening, before leaving early due to a migraine.\par
Just as the distant Layla was vanishing into the woods, the servant reappeared, saying, \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry, Your Grace. Count Klein has already gone home.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I see.\rdblquote  Matthias nodded and smiled. Good work, anyway.\rdblquote  He sauntered back inside.\par
Only then was the servant able to resume his earlier task. He went to Kyle and said something to him.\par
As Kyle listened, a look of dismay spread across his face. He finally asked to be excused and rushed out to the terrace. This didn\rquote t bother Matthias. He knew that Layla was quite a fast walker, despite her size. Kyle would never be able to catch up with her.\par
Matthias gladly accepted a glass of champagne that Riette offered him. It was a perfect party on a beautiful summer night.\par
