The four days Layla spent alone were very strange.\par
Even though she had laughed it off at first, she followed her uncle\rquote s advice and both locked all the windows and took his hunting rifle and kept it by her bed. Despite having taken these precautions before going to bed, she found herself unable to sleep soundly. Sometimes she would be startled awake at the sound of owls and other nocturnal birds. When she did sleep, she often had nightmares. Amid gruesome scenarios where she was being beaten or abandoned, the duke\rquote s face would often flash into her mind\rquote s eye. This would cause the dream to descend into a reenactment of their horrible kiss.\par
Eventually the sun would rise, leading to days that seemed to go on forever. She kept herself busy, never resting throughout her waking hours. She took care of the livestock, tended to the vegetable garden, and swept and dusted the already-pristine cottage. She laundered the curtains and bedclothes,\par
\par
and even tidied the shed. Strangely, she couldn\rquote t read books or study like she normally liked to. When she tried, she couldn\rquote t keep her focus on the words on the page.\par
The days alone were unbearably long. When she couldn\rquote t think of anything else to do, she would pace around until nightfall, which brought with it more nightmares. On the morning of the fourth day, she tied her apron tightly around her waist and started walking out to the backyard, then stopped in place as a sudden thought struck her.\par
It was strange. Bill had always spent most of each day away from the cottage, working, during which time Layla had passed the time alone doing chores and homework. .lust because they lived together didn\rquote t mean that they did everything together. They just sat at the same table, talked about their days under the lamplight of the warm cottage, and\'85\par
\ldblquote This is the first lime,\rdblquote  she muttered lo herself at the sudden realization.\par
Since being sent out of Lovita to Berg, she had never spent a day without seeing Uncle Bill. He had always been nearby. Thanks to him, she had never spent a day alone. At the beginning and end of each day, Uncle Bill had always been there.\par
But now, she was alone.\par
It had taken her four days to realize this, but another realization soon followed. She realized just how lonely, sad, and scary her days wandering the world as a young girl alone had been.\par
After pondering over these things for a while, she kept walking to the backyard. As she fed the chickens and milked the goats, she couldn\rquote t help glancing across the yard repeatedly.\par
Her wish for Uncle Bill\rquote s return had turned into an ardent prayer. She felt that once he returned everything would be all right again. The sadness of\par
\par
haring to become an adult, the traumatic memory of her first kiss, all these confusing and daunting thoughts would disappear once she was no longer alone.\par
