Seeing Layla shaking the jar of colorful candies, her mother had nodded and replied, \ldblquote Yes, I will. \rdblquote\par
If you were going to abandon me, you shouldn\rquote t have answered that way Layla thought as she continued to walk down the road.\par
Her mother had been reputed to be too beautiful to be a poor man\rquote s wife. She fled, leaving her husband and daughter behind like they were pieces of clothing that didn\rquote t fit.\par
Various rumors spread about what became of her. Some said that she had become the mistress of a high-ranking nobleman. Others said that she had married a rich merchant and moved abroad. Whatever had truly become of her, she had cruelly left behind a neglected young daughter, and a husband who was devastated by her abandonment. Haring previously always been a kind, loving father, he started getting drunk every day. He no longer wanted to look at Layla, saying that he couldn\rquote t bear to look at a face that reminded him of the wife who abandoned him.\par
Layla ate the candies sparingly, awaiting her mother\rquote s return. Once the candies had dwindled enough for the bottom of the jar to be risible, she realized that her mother was never going to return. But she didn\rquote t want to accept that painful fact. So, she held off from eating the very last piece of candy.\par
\par
She still had that last piece of candy the day that she was left all alone in the world. Her father, who had grown increasingly sick and debilitated by alcohol, finally passed away. That day, perhaps sensing that his end was near, he turned towards the daughter he had so long avoided and smiled. \ldblquote When the flowers are in bloom, let\rquote s go to the park, Layla, \rdblquote he said in a feeble voice.\par
That cheerful proposition turned out to be his last words, as he died that evening.\par
If you \rquote re going to leave, you shouldn\rquote t make any promises.\par
Unsure what to do with this inconvenient little girl, Layla\rquote s relatives at first stalled, each hoping another would step forward to take care of her. Meanwhile, she was left alone in an empty house, with nothing but an empty promise and a single piece of candy.\par
To this day, she still vividly remembered the shiny, blue piece of candy.\par
After a few days, when she had nothing else left to eat, she had finally put the piece of candy in her mouth. She bit down on it hard, breaking it into shards that scraped against the tender inside of her mouth.\par
Soon the salty taste of blood mingled with the sweet flavor of the candy, but she kept crunching away at it, sobbing all the while. It was a sweet and salty sadness. Looking out the window, she had seen that the flowers were blooming. Carried by the spring breeze, the pale-pink petals fluttered through the air like snowflakes.\par
