\ldblquote If he was going to end his service this year, why did he say before that he was going to extend it for another year?\rdblquote  Countess Brandt grumbled, barely trying to hide her irritation.\par
She had only suggested that they postpone the wedding by a year out of respect for Duke Herhardt\rquote s decision to continue his military service. If she had known how things were actually going to turn out, she would have seen to it that the wedding happened before the end of the summer.\par
\ldblquote I didn\rquote t realize that Matthias was so capricious.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Even though the wedding has been postponed, it\rquote s still nice that he\rquote s returning to Arvis early, Mother,\rdblquote  Claudine replied, not looking disappointed in the least.\par
\ldblquote You certainly seem relaxed about the whole thing. Meanwhile, I can\rquote t help but feel upset that my daughter\rquote s wedding won\rquote t be happening this year.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Why are you upset? Rather than having a rushed, slapdash ceremony, won\rquote t it be better to hold one later on, so that we can have enough time to prepare everything just right?\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote A year is a long time, Claudine. Don\rquote t you realize all that can happen in that time?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I don\rquote t know, Mother. Think back on the last year. It went by in a flash, and nothing much happened. This next year will just be a repeat of that. And anyway, with Duke Herhardt living at Arvis full-time, the wedding preparations will go even more smoothly.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Claudine, men cannot be trusted. And unlike a marriage, an engagement is not a binding agreement.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote We\rquote re not talking about just any man, Mother. We\rquote re talking about Matthias von Herhardt.\rdblquote  Claudine was calmly smiling now. \ldblquote He will never love anyone other than himself.\rdblquote\par
Claudine might have been feeling just as uneasy as her mother was if she hadn\rquote t heard the news that Layla and the doctor\rquote s son were going to be married. With Layla Llewellyn out of the picture, she saw no need for the excessive concern her mother was displaying.\par
\ldblquote For him, this engagement is also a matter of family pride. There\rquote s nothing that can get in the way of that,\rdblquote  she said decisively as she threaded the needle through her embroidery frame.\par
Kind yet cold-hearted, this was how Matthias had behaved ever since he was a child. While Claudine\rquote s other cousins had played pranks on her and called her names, Matthias had always been polite and kind to her. This had been nice, but it had also made him seem more distant and difficult to relate to.\par
Sometimes she wondered if he even had emotions at all. She found it hard to picture him angry, sad, happy, or worried. It seemed more likely that he would look upon the world with the same graceful yet arrogant smile till the day he died. And this was exactly what she wanted out of him.\par
\par
\ldblquote I should visit Arvis next week,\rdblquote  she said, standing up from the sofa feeling refreshed. \ldblquote I can see Duke Herhardt, and I can congratulate my friend on her upcoming marriage. Two birds with one stone.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote A friend with an upcoming wedding? Oh no, you mean that orphan girl the gardener is taking care of? Are you really calling her your friend?\rdblquote  Countess Brandt asked, looking aghast.\par
Claudine flashed a carefree smile. \ldblquote Layla is my friend. My old friend. I should get a wedding gift for her.\rdblquote\par
