After lengthy deliberation, Layla put on the white dress she had purchased for her trip to take the exam in Ratse. She neatly braided her hair, then tied it with her favorite ribbon.\par
She had been to the Ettmans\rquote  home before, but she felt even more nervous than she had felt the first time, both because she and Kyle were now engaged, and because his mother had extended the invitation to her this time.\par
\ldblquote How do I look, Uncle Bill?\rdblquote  she asked, walking out of her room.\par
Lazily smoking a cigarette as he sat on the sill of an open window, he chuckled as he looked at her. \ldblquote With marriage ahead of you, I see that you\rquote re more interested in dressing fancy than you ever were before.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s not that, Uncle. I\rquote m just nervous about seeing Mrs. Ettman.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You\rquote ve got nothing to be nervous about. Haven\rquote t you already met her a few times before?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Sure, but tonight is special.\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote Well you look flawless, so don\rquote t worry.\rdblquote  Stubbing out his cigarette, he stood up and smiled warmly at her. \ldblquote Just be yourself, Layla. That\rquote s enough.\rdblquote  He patted her on the shoulder, and his hand felt just as warm as his smile.\par
While she knew his assessment was biased since he loved her so much, she smiled back at him and nodded. Only after calming down thanks to his words did she realize how anxious she had been all day. Just then, Kyle arrived.\par
\ldblquote Kyle, how do I-\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You look perfect,\rdblquote  he confidently replied, not even letting her finish the question. \ldblquote You look like yourself, and that\rquote s enough.\rdblquote  He was warmly smiling, just like Bill. \ldblquote That\rquote s all I ever want from you, Layla.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Hmm\'85 another guy was just telling me the same thing\'85\rdblquote  she replied, giggling as she observed the similarities between Kyle and Bill\rquote s facial expressions and remarks.\par
\ldblquote What? Who? What guy were you talking to?\rdblquote  Kyle asked.\par
\ldblquote Stop with the nonsense and get going, you loafer,\rdblquote  Bill said, slapping Kyle\rquote s back.\par
Kyle stumbled a bit from the unexpected blow, then looked back at Bill with a frown. \ldblquote But Mr. Remmer, some jerk has been talking to Layl-\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m that jerk, you dummy!\rdblquote  \ldblquote So I just think\'85 wait, what?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You ill-mannered loafer, I\rquote m going to have to seriously consider whether it\rquote s a good idea to let you have Layla after all.\rdblquote  Despite his threateningly blunt words, Bill was patting Kyle\rquote s shoulder very gently and warmly.\par
Layla watched from a few steps back as the two men squabbled.\par
Those two men were the two people she loved most in the whole world. The two most precious people to her. The two people she never wanted to\par
\par
lose. A smile slowly formed on her face and joy suffused her heart. Uncle Bill and Kyle always had that effect on her. She couldn\rquote t imagine life without them. This was why she had finally decided to marry Kyle, after hesitating for so long. With each day that he had proposed to her and she had rejected him, things had grown more awkward between them. If she couldn\rquote t accept his love, then it was only right for a distance to form between them. He had tried as hard as he could to close that gap, but since he had already proposed to her, there were only two options remaining to them; get married or stop seeing\par
each other entirely.\par
Her wish for them to remain good friends, while becoming no more than that, had become impossible the moment he had first proposed to her. Deep down she had known this, and it had pained her throughout the previous autumn, winter, and spring.\par
Marriage, and all the rest of the future life that Kyle had talked to her about, had seemed like a vague, far-off notion to her. She didn\rquote t like the idea of excessive desire either. But if marriage was the only way she could avoid losing Kyle, she thought she could find the courage for it.\par
She had made this resolution in the late spring, the same day that Duke Herhardt had insulted her and made her cry. Not wanting to hold onto anything he had given her, she had thrown all his gold coins into the fountain. The moment she had looked into Kyle\rquote s eyes after he came looking for her, she had made up her mind.\par
The look in his eyes had made her forget all her pain and sorrow. It reminded her of the cozy light of the cottage. So many times, walking down the dark forest path at night, she had seen the warm light of the cottage and had felt deep reassurance knowing that Bill was there waiting for her, ready to comfort her from whatever pain or sadness the world had inflicted on her.\par
\par
That evening by the fountain, Kyle\rquote s eyes had given her the same warm, cozy feeling.\par
As always, Kyle and Bill\rquote s meaningless squabble ended with both of them laughing heartily. Still chuckling, Kyle turned and extended his hand towards her. \ldblquote Let\rquote s go, Layla.\rdblquote\par
Hesitating slightly, she lightly grasped the tip of his fingers. He chuckled and regripped her hand fully. Her tiny hand was practically swallowed up by his.\par
While she still wasn\rquote t used to these new displays of affection that had started after she had accepted his proposal, she didn\rquote t shake off his hand. She didn\rquote t want to lose him.\par
