Mrs. Ettman welcomed her guest with a table packed with delicious- looking dishes and beautiful decorations. Not only Layla, but even Dr.\par
Ettman and Kyle were amazed by the warm reception.\par
\ldblquote Thank you for inviting me, Mrs. Ettman,\rdblquote  Layla said. As she recited this greeting, which she had practiced dozens of times throughout the day, she offered Mrs. Ettman the gifts she had brought; a bouquet of fresh roses, and an ornate jar filled with pickled peaches.\par
\ldblquote Why thank you,\rdblquote  Mrs. Ettman said, accepting the gifts.\par
Layla smiled, feeling relieved that this first step had gone well. Seeing the smile on her face, Kyle allowed a smile to break through his serious demeanor. When Mrs. Ettman noticed that her son was smiling, her face went cold momentarily, before returning to an expression of warm hospitality.\par
\par
Dr. Ettman had said that he would allow Kyle and Layla to marry if they both passed the college entrance examination. There was no doubt that Kyle would pass, and very little doubt that Layla would as well, so their marriage seemed as good as approved already.\par
I can\rquote t believe that my son is going to marry a girl like that, Mrs. Ettman thought, clenching her fists under the table. She wasn\rquote t happy with the situation, but she had decided to stop resisting. She knew her husband and son well enough to realize that too much resistance could backfire. She took a deep breath to regain her composure, then looked at Layla with even more geniality and said, \ldblquote I hope you enjoy your meal, Layla. I made sure to include lots of dishes that you like.\rdblquote\par
Layla, who had been staring at the full table in amazement, smiled when she heard that the food had been planned with her in mind.\par
Looking at Layla\rquote s pretty face, Mrs. Ettman started to feel a strange resentment bubbling up inside of her. You used that pretty smile to seduce my Son, didn\rquote t you? Realizing with a chill the extreme to which her negative thoughts had reached, she quickly lifted her water glass to her lips. The water felt extremely cold going down her throat.\par
\ldblquote Thank you so much,\rdblquote  Layla said with utter sincerity before flashing another polite smile.\par
She was a kind, well-behaved girl. Mrs. Ettman was well aware of this fact, and it had become yet another reason why she didn\rquote t look forward to having her as a daughter-in-law.\par
She would have greatly preferred it if there were some substantial problem with her, some excuse to dislike or even hate her. Then at least she wouldn\rquote t feel so much shame for disliking her in spite of all her virtues.\par
\par
In fact, she disliked Layla for her virtues. She hated that she was so nice, she hated that she was so smart, and she hated that she came from such pitiful circumstances.\par
Noticing that the smile was fading from his wife\rquote s face, Dr. Ettman decided to change the subject. \ldblquote Layla, there\rquote s a professor at Ratse University named Dr. Lorenz who\rquote s an expert in avian biology. Make sure to sign up for a class with him when the school year starts. I think it could be quite beneficial for you to study under a great scholar like him.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Darling, you\rquote re talking as though Layla had already been admitted to the university,\rdblquote  Mrs. Ettman suddenly interjected.\par
Dr. Ettman and Kyle looked at her with puzzled expressions. \ldblquote There\rquote s no way she won\rquote t be admitted,\rdblquote  Dr. Ettman replied. \ldblquote Exactly, Mom. Layla definitely passed the test.\rdblquote\par
The father and son resembled each other strikingly, not only in their voices and facial expressions, but also in the care they were showing for Layla. Mrs. Ettman had to stop herself from delivering the harsh retort that she wanted to. Instead, she once again smiled tenderly and replied, \ldblquote Oh, I see. That makes sense since I know Layla is such a smart girl.\rdblquote\par
Layla, sitting across the table from Mrs. Ettman, smiled bashfully at the compliment.\par
When it came to Layla\rquote s early life in Lovita, Bill never told anyone anything. Other residents of Arvis knew only that as a young girl she had lost her parents and been passed around between relatives in Lovita, before finally being sent to Berg.\par
Linda Ettman hated this about her more than anything, the fact that she had no proper relatives to raise her and care for her, and the fact that she was kicked out of home after home, before finally being sent to a foreign country.\par
\par
She almost shuddered when she thought of what a bad foundation that would be for her when starting a new family with Kyle.\par
It\rquote s not like I\rquote m asking for much, Mrs. Ettman thought.\par
If Layla had simply come from an ordinary family, Mrs. Ettman wouldn\rquote t have been so vehemently opposed to her marrying her son. But given Layla\rquote s lack of a stable family growing up, she worried that she would be unable to become a good wife or mother. She also looked down on what she saw as Layla\rquote s greedy desire to go to college. The way Mrs. Ettman saw it, if Layla wanted this, despite being both female and poor, then she was likely greedy in all sorts of other ways. She thus highly doubted that her son could be happy with her as his wife.\par
I\rquote ve got to stop it, Mrs. Ettman thought, clasping her hands together under the table. I\rquote ve got to stop this marriage from happening, no matter what it takes.\par
