Layla\rquote s college acceptance letter was delivered by the very same mailman who had delivered Layla herself to Arvis several years earlier. Layla was out on a walk when the letter arrived, so Bill received it on her behalf. As soon as he saw the envelope, he went silent and started staring into space.\par
\ldblquote Mr. Remmer?\rdblquote  the mailman said, looking at Bill with concern after he made no response to his words of congratulation. Bill\rquote s carefree expression was gone, and now his face was starting to flush. \ldblquote Are you all right, Mr. Remmer?\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote Oh\'85 yes. Nothing to worry about,\rdblquote  Bill replied, rubbing his eyes with his big hand. \ldblquote I was just lost in thought for a moment,\rdblquote  he added in a louder voice. His eyes were moist with tears.\par
The mailman was already aware of how tenderhearted Bill was beneath his gruff exterior, so he pretended not to notice the tears. Nodding, he said, \ldblquote Well anyway, congratulations, truly. I\rquote m so happy to hear that Layla will be attending the empire\rquote s most prestigious university.\rdblquote  With those additional words of congratulation, he left.\par
Bill returned to the porch, holding the precious letter of acceptance. Sitting in his chair, he read the letter from start to finish a few more times, then caressed it with his fingers. The excited flush gradually left his face after he took a few deep breaths.\par
Just then, Layla returned. \ldblquote Uncle Bill!\rdblquote  she shouted, waving and running towards him as soon as she saw him. The old leather bag on her shoulder bounced wildly as she ran.\par
\ldblquote That stupid bag,\rdblquote  Bill muttered, chuckling to himself.\par
It was an old tool bag that he had given her the summer she had first come to Arvis. Even after acquiring a few other, nicer bags, when going out into the forest she always grabbed that worn-out old thing.\par
\ldblquote When are you planning on throwing away that piece of junk?\rdblquote  he grumbled at her as she sat down next to him.\par
\ldblquote Throw it away?! It still works for me just fine,\rdblquote  she replied. \ldblquote Please, just throw it away. Put it out of its misery already.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s still got some kick in it. I\rquote m going to keep using it a little longer.\rdblquote  She smiled as she ran her fingers along the bag\rquote s tattered strap. \ldblquote I\rquote m going to miss it when it\rquote s gone.\rdblquote\par
\par
Rather than telling her how silly she sounded, he decided to stop talking about the bag, and instead held out the acceptance letter in front of her face.\par
\ldblquote What\rquote s this, Uncle Bill?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Read it and find out.\rdblquote\par
She gave him a surprised glance, then took the letter. Rather than bursting into ecstatic celebration as he had expected her to, as she read the letter she seemed to grow calmer and more relaxed.\par
Eventually, he grew concerned enough by her unusually calm reaction that he called to her. \ldblquote Layla\'85?\rdblquote\par
Only then did she lift her head and look at him. She had a reserved smile on her face.\par
It would be nice if you acted a bit more like a young lady at times like this, Bill thought, scratching the back of his neck in embarrassment over the fact that she had reacted to the news in a more adult-like manner than he had.\par
Watching his reaction, she started smiling even more brightly. Before he even had time to acknowledge her smile, she had wrapped her arms around him joyfully.\par
\ldblquote No need to overreact,\rdblquote  he said. Despite his indifferent words, however, he lovingly patted her on the back.\par
After a while, she looked up at him and whispered, \ldblquote Thank you.\rdblquote  Her eyes were welling up with tears. \ldblquote Thank you so much, Uncle Bill.\rdblquote  She smiled brightly at him. \ldblquote It\rquote s all thanks to you, Uncle Bill.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote That\rquote s ridiculous,\rdblquote  he replied. Taking a deep breath, he tried to distract himself by mentally counting up all the tasks he had to finish before sundown. Even so, his eyes felt like they were ready to burst into tears again. Why is she so good at making me cry? \ldblquote You\rquote re the one who studied, you\rquote re the one who did so well on the exam. What did I do?\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote No. No, Uncle Bill.\rdblquote  Shaking her head vigorously, she grabbed his hand, barely able to fit both her hands around one of his. \ldblquote I\'85\rdblquote  Her hands felt soft and warm as they gripped his. \ldblquote If it weren\rquote t for you, Uncle Bill, I would have\'85\rdblquote  On this wonderful day, she looked like she was about to start sobbing. This frightened Bill more than anything. He didn\rquote t like seeing her shedding tears,\par
even if they were tears of joy.\par
He only ever wanted to see a cheerful smile on her face. All the love and care he had put into all the flowers and trees he had cultivated throughout his whole life, didn\rquote t equal the love he felt for this girl. There was no flower or tree that he cherished more than her.\par
He wasn\rquote t sure when it had gotten that way. One day he had simply realized that it was true.\par
Clearing his throat to calm his emotions, he said, \ldblquote Let\rquote s go to the capital next weekend, what do you say?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You and me, to Ratse?\rdblquote  she asked, looking surprised.\par
\ldblquote Now that you\rquote ve been admitted to the university, you\rquote ve got to pay the tuition and enroll in classes, right? While we\rquote re there, we can do some sightseeing. It\rquote ll be nice. After all, I\rquote ve never taken you anywhere before.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Are you serious?\rdblquote  she asked. Her tear- filled eyes were glistening with anticipation. \ldblquote You and I are going on a trip? Really?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote It won\rquote t be a fancy trip or anything. Mainly we\rquote re just going to pay the tuition.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Even so.\rdblquote\par
As he saw how excited she was about the trip, he felt regret. It was then that Chef Mona\rquote s words about him not knowing how to take care of a daughter hit home. He wondered why he had never taken her on any sort of trip, even a short trip somewhere close where she could see some sights and he\par
\par
could buy her some good food to eat. He wondered why this thought was only now occurring to him, when she would be moving away so soon.\par
\ldblquote The tuition is a lot of money, and anyway I\rquote m not comfortable sending you and Kyle off together without a chaperone again, even if you\rquote re going to get married. So I have no choice but to bring you myself\'85\rdblquote  He continued to mutter excuses until he felt embarrassed and laughed at himself.\par
Layla hugged him again.\par
\ldblquote Hey, Layla, I was right, wasn\rquote t I?\rdblquote  he said, softly patting her head with a delighted smile on his face. \ldblquote Didn\rquote t I say you would become an outstanding adult?\rdblquote  Although this comment failed to fully convey his emotions, he gave up on trying to find more words and quietly continued patting her head. To prevent himself from being overcome by sobs, he started mentally counting up his tasks once again.\par
