Word spread quickly through Arvis that Layla, the girl that Bill Remmer was raising, had gotten into Ratse University.\par
Simultaneously, the word was spreading that Dr. Ettman\rquote s only son had been admitted into the same university\rquote s medical school after passing the entrance exam with flying colors, but this news didn\rquote t generate as much buzz, since everyone had already expected it to happen.\par
Layla\rquote s news was a different story, however. For several days, whenever two or more people met, Layla Llewellyn would inevitably become a topic of their conversation. This was even true of conversations that went on in the duke\rquote s greenhouse.\par
\ldblquote To think, a common orphan, and a female one at that, going to college. Bill Remmer must have done an amazing job raising her,\rdblquote  said Dowager\par
\par
Herhardt. Normally she wouldn\rquote t participate in gossiping about a lowly child, but today she was displaying just as much interest in Layla as anyone.\par
\ldblquote She must have been born lucky. First, she met her savior, Bill, and then she managed to get engaged to Dr. Ettman\rquote s son,\rdblquote  Elise von Herhardt added.\par
Claudine, who had been quietly sipping her tea, politely smiled and nodded. \ldblquote I\rquote m so happy that poor girl has had such good luck.\rdblquote  She felt more capable than ever of sincerely praising Layla.\par
Just then, a maid that Dowager Herhardt had instructed to go to the cottage returned, accompanied by Layla, who was very neatly dressed.\par
\ldblquote Come here and have a seat,\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt calmly said.\par
Layla\rquote s eyes were wide with surprise, as were those of Lady Herhardt and Claudine.\par
\ldblquote There\rquote s nothing wrong with offering a cup of tea to a special girl, is there?\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt asked, smiling. While all the Herhardts were aloof, blue-blooded aristocrats, Katharina von Herhardt was especially so. Thus, it came as a shock to everyone that she would invite an orphan girl raised by a gardener to tea.\par
With a nervous look on her face, Layla sat in the seat that the maid guided her to.\par
As soon as Layla\rquote s tea had been served, Dowager Herhardt began. \ldblquote So, I heard that you have been admitted into a university that even sons from the most distinguished families have trouble getting into.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s all thanks to Uncle Bill,\rdblquote  Layla replied, her eyes lowered politely. \ldblquote Of course. We mustn\rquote t forget your savior, Bill Remmer.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Indeed, Ma\rquote am.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I hear you\rquote re from Lovita.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes, Ma\rquote am. My Mother was from Lovita, but my Father was from Berg.\rdblquote\par
\par
\ldblquote You and I are alike in that respect.\rdblquote\par
Elise and Claudine reacted with shock at this casual comment.\par
Dowager Herhardt\rquote s father had been a prestigious marquess who was related to the Berg imperial family, while her mother came from the Lovita royal family\rquote s bloodline. It was astounding that she was comparing her lineage to that of a poor orphan girl.\par
\ldblquote Tell me something that you would like to receive,\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt said, setting down her teacup.\par
Layla was taken aback by this sudden command.\par
\ldblquote Bill Remmer is one of my favorite employees. You\rquote re like a daughter to him, so I would like to give you a congratulatory gift,\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt calmly said.\par
Out of surprise, Layla forgot her manners and stared straight at Dowager Herhardt. Meanwhile, two more people entered the greenhouse. \ldblquote Duke Herhardt!\rdblquote  Claudine exclaimed with delight.\par
Everyone looked in the direction of Claudine\rquote s gaze, including Layla.\par
Matthias von Herhardt was standing by the table. He made eye contact with Layla, whereupon they both frowned for different reasons. There was a slight feeling of tension in the room, until Layla removed it by averting her eyes.\par
\ldblquote This girl has been admitted into Ratse University. We wanted to congratulate her, so we invited her to have a cup of tea with us,\rdblquote  Elise von Herhardt said, smiling as she explained the unusual situation.\par
Matthias nodded and sat down next to Claudine, in a seat that just happened to be right across from Layla\rquote s.\par
\ldblquote You\rquote re back early,\rdblquote  Claudine said to her fiance, smiling at him tenderly.\par
\par
Now that he had taken full charge of the family\rquote s business, Matthias usually left the mansion early in the morning and didn\rquote t return until late at night. It had already been a week since Claudine had arrived for her visit to Arvis, but this was the first time she had seen him return home while it was still light out.\par
\ldblquote The meeting ended earlier than expected, My Lady,\rdblquote  he replied.\par
\ldblquote That\rquote s good to hear. I\rquote ve been worried that you\rquote ve been overworking yourself lately.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Claudine is right, Matthias,\rdblquote  Elise von Herhardt remarked. \ldblquote You shouldn\rquote t overdo things when it comes to work, or it could have an impact on your health.\rdblquote\par
The conversation at the tea table then turned to Matthias\rquote s situation and the family business. In an instant, it was as if Layla was no longer there.\par
Thanks to this, she was able to catch her breath and take a sip of her tea, which by now was lukewarm. She wanted nothing more than to escape this uncomfortable situation, but she couldn\rquote t leave without first being excused, especially with the duke\rquote s mother and grandmother there.\par
She set her cup down on the saucer, trying not to make a sound as she did so. Then she absentmindedly looked up, and was so startled by what she saw, she flinched and pushed her body into the back of her chair. Matthias, sitting between his fiancee and his mother while they chatted, was looking straight at her. His cold eyes looked exactly the same as they had looked the day he had trampled on her heart.\par
Rather than reaching for her cup again, she hurriedly put her hands under the table. Matthias\rquote s gaze then turned to Claudine as she began to speak with him. When Claudine started speaking with Elise again, his gaze returned to Layla.\par
\par
Layla quickly lowered her head, but she could still feel his burdensome gaze on her. Soon, memories from the previous summer began to flood into her mind. Recalling those memories while in Claudine\rquote s presence made her feel even worse. A chill went down her spine. Matthias was the one who had committed the wrongdoings, but Layla was the one who felt guilty.\par
\ldblquote So, have you thought of something you would like to receive?\rdblquote  Dowager Herhardt asked. With this question, the table\rquote s attention returned to Layla.\par
Layla reflexively lifted her head upon hearing her name, then unwittingly started biting her lip. This was because Matthias\rquote s blue eyes were still fixed on her. Trying to hide her anxiety, she quickly turned to face Dowager Herhardt and replied, \ldblquote You have already given me a huge gift, Ma\rquote am. I don\rquote t need anything more.\par
\ldblquote You already received a gift?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes. Simply by allowing me to stay at Uncle Bill\rquote s cottage here in Arvis, you\rquote ve done more for me than I\rquote ll ever be able to repay you for. It\rquote s the biggest, most wonderful gift I\rquote ve ever received, and I\rquote m eternally grateful for it.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote All we did was approve Bill\rquote s request.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Your approval of that request was a gift that changed my whole life, Ma\rquote am.\rdblquote  A slight smile formed on her lips. \ldblquote My deepest thanks to you as well, Lady Herhardt,\rdblquote  she said, turning to Elise. \ldblquote And also to you, Your Grace, and you, Miss Claudine.\rdblquote  Since the only thing she really wanted at that moment was to be allowed to leave, which was a request she knew she couldn\rquote t make, she instead expressed her thanks to all these people that she didn\rquote t particularly care for. \ldblquote I\rquote ll never forget the kindness you\rquote ve extended to me, even after I depart from Arvis.\rdblquote  She then bowed her head deeply, to indicate her polite refusal of any further gift.\par
\par
Dowager Herhardt looked closely at her for a moment, then nodded. The act of refusing the Herhardt family\rquote s generosity was bold, but Layla had expressed this refusal in a highly refined manner, not at all what one would expect from a girl of such lowly upbringing.\par
\ldblquote You\rquote re already saying your goodbyes? That makes me sad, Layla,\rdblquote  Claudine said. \ldblquote I understand how you feel, but we would still like to give you a gift. Ah, how about we pay your tuition?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote No thank you, Miss. I\rquote m happy to accept the kindness you\rquote ve already shown me as my gift,\rdblquote  Layla replied, still smiling politely. \ldblquote Uncle Bill has already saved up the money for my tuition. He told me he really wants to pay for my first semester of college.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Really? Then we\rquote ll have to think of something else. I can\rquote t let an old friend leave empty handed, when she\rquote s just gotten into college and gotten engaged.\rdblquote  Smiling, she turned to Matthias. \ldblquote Isn\rquote t that right, Duke Herhardt?\rdblquote  Her voice was as sweet as honey as she gently pressured him.\par
He glanced at Layla for a moment, then lightly nodded his head.\par
After exchanging a few more pleasantries and salutations, Layla was finally free to leave this uncomfortable tea table. As she was walking away from the Herhardts, she was able to take in the interior of the greenhouse.\par
Everyone who visited it praised it enthusiastically, calling it Arvis\rquote s heaven. Now that Layla was able to look around the lavish greenhouse, she felt uneasy. Seeing all the beautiful birds with clipped wings, and all the excessively colorful and fragrant flowers, she started to feel overwhelmed. This feeling was heightened by the sound of the water flowing in the marble fountain, and\par
by the sunlight that was intensified as it shone through the glass walls.\par
She left the greenhouse immediately, not looking back once. Only once she was outside, in the gentle embrace of the breeze and the natural sunlight, was\par
\par
she able to breathe a sigh of relief.\par
Her long evening shadow followed her as she walked back towards the woods.\par
