After finishing his shower, Matthias sauntered towards the window on the west side of his bedroom. He had an orderly daily routine. No matter when he got to sleep, he generally woke up early in the morning. Soon after waking, he headed to the bathroom for a quick shower, and then he began his day. After repeating this routine so many times, it had become second nature to him. Including what he was doing now.\par
He gazed out the window at the lush rose garden below. Naturally, Layla was there. She seemed to be following the gardener around more than usual, perhaps because the day of her departure was drawing near. She and the gardener stayed near each other throughout their work, chatting all the while.\par
He had once thought of her as a girl of extremely few words. When she was around him or Claudine, she generally kept her lips tightly shut.\par
When the scheduled time rolled around, he heard a familiar knock at his door.\par
\ldblquote Your Grace, it\rquote s me, Hessen.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Come in,\rdblquote  he replied, standing with his back to the still-open window. A breeze caused the thin curtains to sway.\par
While Hessen briefed him on the day\rquote s schedule, Matthias read the newspaper he had brought. There was nothing scheduled until lunch.\par
\ldblquote I must admit, Your Grace, Arvis felt a bit empty until you returned,\rdblquote  Hessen remarked, instead of staying silent like usual.\par
\par
Matthias set down his teacup and looked at him. \ldblquote Grandmother and Mother would be sorry to hear you say that.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Pardon? Ah, no, Your Grace. That\rquote s not what I meant. It\rquote s just that-\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I know,\rdblquote  Matthias said. \ldblquote I know what you were getting at.\rdblquote  A brief smile flashed across his face. Even after the smile was gone, he continued looking at his middle aged butler with a gaze as calm as still water.\par
Relieved that his master was not upset, Hessen soon left.\par
Even after the door to his room was shut, Matthias remained leaning against the window frame, reading the newspaper. The sunlight shone upon his eyes, making them appear paler than usual through the hair that hung loosely over his forehead.\par
He put down the newspaper after perusing an article about a company owned by Count Klein, the man with whom he would be having lunch. He slowly turned around and saw Layla, who was now at a flowerbed quite close to the mansion. Underneath her straw hat, which he recalled had barely been rescued from the river, her braided hair swung as she moved.\par
The gardener said something, and she excitedly said something in response. She was laughing. He could tell this even though her face was obscured by the shadow of her hat. She was laughing loudly.\par
He narrowed his eyes and slowly ran his fingers through his hair. Maybe I shouldn\rquote t have come back\'85 he thought. He had felt this the moment he first set foot on the estate.\par
His life had been meticulously planned. It was like there was a staircase leading to the perfect life, and all he had to do was slowly ascend it.\par
But now he had reached a warped step. He still couldn\rquote t understand why he had made that choice, which had gone against his life plan.\par
\par
Or perhaps his first misstep had happened earlier than the night when he had torn the military extension request form in half. Perhaps it was the day he had postponed his wedding, saying that he wanted to extend his military service period by a year, when he had been blinded by desire. Perhaps it was the day he had approached her after she fell from her bicycle. Perhaps it was some earlier day that he didn\rquote t even remember.\par
He still desired her. He was well aware of that. Therefore, he wanted her to disappear. He was well aware of that too.\par
This confusing situation had created a rift between his heated emotions and his ardent desire. He wasn\rquote t sure of the solution, but he trusted that time would resolve things.\par
He closed the window and got dressed. As he strode down the corridor, his face was alternately obscured and illuminated by shadows and light from the windows.\par
Leaving his attendants behind, he exited the mansion alone and took to the forest path leading to the river. The shadows in the woods seemed especially dark because the sun was shining so brightly.\par
He stopped and stood amid those shadows for a moment, musing.\par
He had never previously experienced desire, so he didn\rquote t understand the longing that he was experiencing now. Suddenly, he realized that there was an even stranger feeling; the feeling of being unable to have what he desired.\par
