The closer Daniel Raynor got to the gardener\rquote s cottage, the paler his face grew with trepidation. It wasn\rquote t yet the hottest point in the day, but his forehead was damp with sweat. \ldblquote This is crazy,\rdblquote  he muttered to himself as he caught his first glimpse of the cottage\rquote s roof in the distance.\par
Mrs. Ettman had told him she wanted Daniel to take the college tuition money the gardener had saved up, and hold on to it for a while. In plain words, she wanted him to steal the money.\par
The graceful, high-minded Linda Ettman had persuaded her cousin to commit theft, out of a desire to separate her son from that poor girl.\par
When he had reached the cottage\rquote s front yard, Daniel pulled his handkerchief from his pocket and wiped his face. His other hand was tightly gripping a briefcase.\par
He also felt sorry for Kyle, who had fallen in love with and decided to marry a girl who wasn\rquote t a good fit for him. Everyone had expected that the Ettmans\rquote  daughter in-law would come from at least a low- ranking aristocratic family.\par
But Kyle sincerely wanted to marry Layla, and Dr. Ettman was in support of his choice, feeling that she was a decent person, despite her lowly social status. Mrs. Ettman had agreed on this point. Who could have known the dagger hidden behind her gentle smile?\par
\ldblquote Money is the enemy,\rdblquote  Daniel muttered.\par
\par
Pulling himself out of his low spirits, he strode towards the cottage. Linda had told him that it would be empty during the morning hours. If by some chance Layla was there, he was to tell her that he had just been visiting the Ettmans and was stopping by to congratulate her on getting into college. He and Layla had met before, so it was a plausible excuse.\par
As he approached the front door, he started to hope that she was home, so that he could abandon the whole plan. He knocked and waited, but there was no sound from within the cottage.\par
Abandoning himself to despair, he tried the knob. Just as his cousin had promised, it was unlocked.\par
\ldblquote But Linda, even if the tuition money is stolen, someone else could step forward and offer to cover the expense. Even your husband said he was willing to pay her tuition, \rdblquote he had said to her.\par
Smiling bitterly in response, she had replied, \ldblquote Trust me, I know my h husband better than you, Daniel.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote But why\'85\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote The missing tuition money is just an excuse.\rdblquote  \ldblquote Huh?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote An excuse to break that girl\rquote s heart, \rdblquote  Linda had replied with a sigh.\par
Daniel had asked no further questions. If this was his last hope, he didn\rquote t want to know any more details, since they would likely only make him feel even more guilty and ashamed.\par
I\rquote m just helping my cousin borrow the gardener\rquote s money. He kept repeating this rationalization in his mind as he entered the cottage.\par
It didn\rquote t take him very long to finish. As he stepped out of the cottage, money-filled briefcase in hand, he felt a sense of relief. His part in this scheme\par
\par
was now done. All that was left was for him to deliver the money to Linda, receive his fee, which would save his house from foreclosure, and go home.\par
To avoid running into the gardener, he chose a circuitous route, walking alongside the river. However, this cautious decision plunged him into an even worse predicament. He realized this after seeing a young man on this same path.\par
The young man slowly came to a stop after seeing Daniel. Not seeming particularly startled or alarmed, he simply stared at him.\par
Is he a servant from Arvis? Daniel wondered, feeling some relief. This relief was short-lived, however. Soon he started to feel deep dread as it occurred to him that there was no way that a servant would be out for a leisurely stroll by the river during the busy morning hours. His attire didn\rquote t look like that of a servant either. And moreover, his face looked very familiar.\par
Then it hit him, he had seen that face countless times in the newspaper, and had even seen him in person a few times from a distance.\par
It was Duke Herhardt.\par
