Once again, Layla Llewellyn\rquote s name was on the lips of all the Arvis staff members for several days, but this time for a very different reason. Now\par
it was because the gardener\rquote s cottage had been burglarized.\par
News of this spread throughout the mansion in a flash. Upon first hearing about it, most people doubted that any thief would have dared enter the Herhardt estate, but their doubt was dispelled when they saw the half-crazed Bill Remmer wandering about, looking for the thief.\par
Matthias left the mansion when the commotion about the burglary was reaching its height. He had to depart then so that he wouldn\rquote t be late for his lunch.\par
As his car left the mansion\rquote s tree-lined driveway, he began to sense that whatever was going on wasn\rquote t a matter of trivial gossip. There were policemen\par
\par
on horseback approaching from across the road. Since the road came to a dead end at Arvis, there was no need to guess where they were headed.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m told that a thief broke into the gardener\rquote s home,\rdblquote  his assistant said, anticipating his question.\par
\ldblquote Bill Remmer\rquote s home?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes. It seems that the money he had saved up for his child\rquote s college tuition disappeared, which is the reason for the uproar.\par
A thief. Tuition. Layla. Matthias tilted his head as he recited these words in his mind with fascination. Just as the car was leaving the shade of Platanus Road, he remembered the stranger he had encountered that morning while walking next to the Schulter River.\par
The man had introduced himself, saying that his name was Daniel Raynor and that he was the cousin of Dr. Ettman\rquote s wife. He had explained that he was in town visiting the Ettmans, and had stopped by the Arvis estate to congratulate Layla on getting into college. He had gone on to say various things about the small investment company he ran, international mining rights, the stock market, and other things, but Matthias had mostly stopped listening at this point so he didn\rquote t remember much of what he had said.\par
What he did remember was the time.\par
While the middle-aged man was prattling on with sweat pouring down his forehead, Matthias had glanced at the man\rquote s wristwatch, and noticed that it was not even nine o\rquote clock yet. It was an unusually early time of day for a refined middle-class businessman to be visiting his cousin\rquote s son\rquote s fiancee to congratulate her on getting into college.\par
However, since the matter didn\rquote t concern him, he had let it go, and hadn\rquote t thought anything more of it, until he heard the remarkable news that a thief had broken into the gardener\rquote s cottage.\par
\par
But why?\par
Whatever sort of financial trouble the man might have been in, the thought that he would have sneaked into an unfamiliar place like Arvis and stolen the money of the gardener of all people sounded ridiculous. Why wouldn\rquote t he have simply stolen from his wealthy cousin\rquote s house?\par
Just as he was ready to stop thinking about the strange scenario, he found himself muttering, \ldblquote Linda Ettman.\rdblquote\par
Linda Ettman was the missing link that made the connection between Layla and that man more plausible. It was still just conjecture, but with her in the picture, the pieces seemed to fit together much better, and this intrigued him.\par
The car came to a stop in front of the hotel where the lunch was being held. Before getting out, he instructed his assistant, \ldblquote Find out whatever you can about Daniel Raynor. As quickly as possible.\rdblquote\par
