The food on the dinner table was almost completely untouched. Bill and Kyle normally had great appetites, but not tonight. Of course, Layla knew the reason for this, and eventually, she got up and started quietly clearing the table. With his plate still more than half-full, Bill got up, went to the porch, and lit a cigarette.\par
Since the burglary, the atmosphere in the cottage had been gloomy and morose.\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s all right, Layla,\rdblquote  Kyle said as he helped her clear the table.\par
\par
Her busy hands stopped for a moment and she looked up at him with her green eyes.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m sure they\rquote ll catch the thief,\rdblquote  he said. \ldblquote Yeah.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote And even if not, you don\rquote t need to worry about tuition. My dad wanted me to tell you that he\rquote ll cover it.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Kyle.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t even think about refusing. He wanted to cover your tuition from the start. He only gave in after Bill kept insisting on paying for it himself.\rdblquote  Kyle was speaking more assertively than normal. \ldblquote We\rquote re getting married, Layla. That means we\rquote re family. You don\rquote t need to worry about being indebted to my dad. Family members help each other out, it\rquote s the most natural thing in the world. Right?\rdblquote\par
With her eyes lowered, she stood motionless for a short while, then slowly nodded. He looked at her face, which had grown noticeably gaunt in just a few days, and he felt bitter hatred towards the thief who had disturbed the peacefulness of the forest.\par
\ldblquote Anyway, for now, let\rquote s assume they\rquote re going to catch the thief.\rdblquote  He was well aware that the chances of the thief being caught were very low, but he wanted to retain a sense of hope for her sake.\par
After this comment, she looked at him with a hopeful smile. \ldblquote Yeah. Thanks, Kyle.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Thanks for what? What did I do?\rdblquote  \ldblquote Just thanks. Thanks for everything.\rdblquote\par
She smiled again, but somehow it only made his heart feel heavier.\par
He vividly remembered the look of ebullient joy on her face when she had told him that she was going on a trip to the capital with Uncle Bill. The\par
\par
purpose of the trip was to pay her tuition, but in reality, it was going to be the first vacation they had gone on together. She had been as happy as a child as she went on about it, saying that since it would be her second trip to the capital, she would be able to act as his guide. Kyle had teased her, but he had been just as happy as she was.\par
She had gushed forth about the places she would go with Uncle Bill, the food she would eat with him, and the things she would do with him. Hearing her talk so excitedly, Kyle had almost started to envy Bill.\par
However, the thief had spoiled everything. Even if the money were recovered, it seemed impossible that Layla and Bill could enjoy a happy, carefree vacation like they had planned. And Kyle knew there was nothing he could do about this.\par
He took a deep breath and let it out to shake off his deep feeling of hopelessness, then walked out to the porch and sat next to Bill, who had a sullen look on his face. Bill glanced at him, then lit another cigarette. He had spent the days since the burglary seething inside and coming up with dozens of ways to kill someone. Now, however, his anger had given way to dejection. After a while, he spoke. \ldblquote It\rquote s all my fault.\rdblquote  His voice sounded weak and dispirited. \ldblquote I was storing all that money at home, without even bothering to lock the door.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s not your fault,\rdblquote  Kyle replied. \ldblquote Who would ever think a thief would\rquote ve sneaked into Arvis?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Next week is the deadline for tuition, and I\rquote m not sure if that miserable thief will be caught by then.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t worry about that. If there\rquote s no progress in the search by then, my father said that he will cover Layla\rquote s tuition. I told Layla already.\rdblquote\par
Bill\rquote s eyes sank as he looked at Kyle\rquote s smiling face.\par
\par
\ldblquote My father is going to call the police station tomorrow,\rdblquote  Kyle continued. \ldblquote He has a friend there, and he\rquote s going to ask him to do a thorough investigation into this incident.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Thanks, Kyle. I\rquote m so indebted to your family, I feel ashamed.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m sorry to hear you feel that way. I still don\rquote t think I\rquote ve made up for half the food I\rquote ve eaten over here.\rdblquote\par
Bill chuckled weakly at this remark. \ldblquote Please give your parents my thanks. No, actually, tell them I\rquote ll pay them a visit once this matter is resolved.\rdblquote  He squeezed Kyle\rquote s shoulder.\par
Kyle thought about saying that there was no need to go to the trouble of a visit, but then changed his mind and slowly nodded. He wasn\rquote t sure, but he had a feeling that the visit would be more for Bill than for his parents, and he didn\rquote t want to disappoint him.\par
