The leisurely weekend morning felt particularly long and dreary. After getting dressed, he looked out the window again while sipping coffee. Bill Remmer and his helpers were busily working, but Layla was still nowhere to be seen. He started to wonder if he had been too greedy in desiring someone so pathetic.\par
Feeling the urge to mock her, he set out on a light morning walk. The servants didn\rquote t interfere, knowing that he enjoyed going for a swim in the river by his cabin when he had some spare time.\par
He hoped that Layla would be crying so that he could have a good time again. As he reflected on that wish, he arrived at the gardener\rquote s cottage. Normally, Layla would be reading a book on the porch or doing chores in the yard, but she was nowhere in sight. The area around the cottage was completely still and quiet.\par
Just as he was starting to feel annoyed, a white pigeon flew down. It was flying straight towards the window on the back side of the cottage as if this was its destination.\par
He reflexively walked in the direction it had flown. The pigeon was sitting calmly on the sill of the half-open window. He noticed that it had a small note tied to its leg. Just then, he realized that the window was to Layla\rquote s room.\par
He approached, but the pigeon didn\rquote t fly away. Is it a messenger pigeon?\par
\par
Furrowing his brow, he calmly reached out of grabbed the bird. It remained docile even in his hand. He stared at the note the bird had brought until he was startled by the sound of crying coming through the window.\par
It turned out that the room he had thought was empty actually contained Layla, lying on her bed, sobbing miserably.\par
