Even though she knew it was time to become an adult, she had been ready to make the most childish choice one could make. Out of a desire not to lose Kyle, with the added bonus of being able to go to college, she had accepted his proposal. She now felt deep shame and guilt over that choice.\par
Mrs. Ettman had been right. The moment she had accepted his proposal, her relationship with Kyle had crossed a line of no return.\par
Mrs. Ettman hated her for doing that, and she didn\rquote t have it in her to endure her hatred. She didn\rquote t want to spoil Kyle\rquote s image of his mother by telling him what she had done. She didn\rquote t want to hurt Uncle Bill either.\par
There was no going back now. Revealing the truth about the theft would do nothing but hurt them. This was what she had realized after shedding enough tears to drain all the water out of her body.\par
Therefore, she had to turn away from him. Right then, in silence. That was the only way to protect her self-respect, Kyle\rquote s family, and Bill\rquote s feelings.\par
\ldblquote Let\rquote s keep our old memories untainted, Kyle.\rdblquote  She looked into his eyes and quietly spoke. \ldblquote Let\rquote s do that.\rdblquote\par
Let\rquote s leave our wonderful childhood days behind up and become adults, she wanted to add. Instead, she let a quiet sigh pass through her parched lips. Don\rquote t cry, she thought to herself. She had said the phrase to herself over and over as she had walked to the stream. Fortunately, it worked, and no tears fell from her eyes. She closed her eyes and started walking.\par
He was standing in a daze. As she passed by him, he grabbed her wrist. \ldblquote Don\rquote t go.\rdblquote  His large, hot hand was trembling.\par
\par
She opened her eyes and, instead of answering him, simply pushed his hand away. Despite his physical strength, his hand weakly fell off her wrist at the slightest touch.\par
She walked away with calm, light steps, leaving him behind, while he gazed into the stream. Her eyes were moist, but she didn\rquote t shed a single tear until she arrived back at the cottage.\par
