\ldblquote Everything has been taken care of in accordance with your instructions, Your Grace,\rdblquote  Hessen said after finishing a short phone call.\par
Matthias slowly nodded, as if he understood what Hessen meant without need for further explanation. His gaze was fixed on the river visible through the large window.\par
\ldblquote They will send a telegram later today,\rdblquote  Hessen added, thus concluding his report. He then discussed several other topics.; the guest list for the dinner scheduled the following week, Count and Countess Brandt\rquote s upcoming visit, staff hiring issues, and the like. Each new topic was met with a concise response from Matthias.\par
\ldblquote I\rquote ll take my leave then, Your Grace,\rdblquote\par
Hessen said, having reached the end of his list of issues to report. He bowed and left.\par
Matthias, now alone in the cabin, lazily walked down the steps to the first floor. The cabin was built as a sort of houseboat, floating on the water. The boathouse took up half of the cabin\rquote s first story and let out directly into the river. To cross the river, all one had to do was unmoor one of the boats floating in the boathouse and start rowing.\par
Standing on the floor of the boathouse, Matthias stripped off his clothes and jumped into the water. As he swam through the boathouse\rquote s arch-shaped exit, the sunlight glistened on his naked body.\par
\par
He slowly swam, letting the current carry him. With his supple swimming technique, he looked like he was part of the river.\par
It was only natural that his fixation on things he couldn\rquote t have would grow and intensify. He knew this to be true, even though he also knew that obtaining the object of his desire would cause it to lose its appeal for him. He knew it was no different with her.\par
He came to this conclusion as he gasped for air. He knew it was silly for him to have gotten swept up by a fleeting covetousness, but he still graciously accepted his desire to have her. And now he could have her all he wanted. After failing to protect his first love, the boy had gone and left Layla behind. Everything in Matthias\rquote s world was in its right place, in perfect order.\par
By the time he turned and began swimming back to the cabin, the sun had started to set. As he gazed at the familiar tree by the river\rquote s edge, his eyes narrowed. He could hardly believe it, but she was there.\par
Layla.\par
He quietly repeated her name, sending ripples over the surface of the water.\par
