The curtains half-covering the open window in the bedroom ballooned into the room and slowly settled back down several times with the breeze. The quiet piano piece flowed in a similar rhythm. The playing was both delicate and showy, and at times temperamental.\par
Matthias was sitting in a chair by the window, holding a handkerchief and a pair of tin scissors. He snapped his fingers, and his canary flew over and perched on his hand at once. Even birdsong seemed to be amenable to training, as the canary\rquote s song had grown more beautiful over time.\par
Matthias smiled at the bird, which seemed to be singing in response to the piano music. It shook its tiny body with its soft feathers and cocked its head as if deep in thought.\par
When it stopped singing, Matthias gently wrapped it in the handkerchief. The bird keeper always covered the birds\rquote  eyes before clipping their wings so that they wouldn\rquote t know who had clipped them and develop a fear of humans.\par
Matthias deftly spread the feathers of the obedient bird\rquote s wing. For a while, he had left the task of wing-clipping to the bird keeper, but by now he had learned to do it himself. The first few times he tried it, he clipped them too short and the bird bled. Luckily it was only a few drops of blood, but he didn\rquote t like seeing its golden wings stained red, so he learned to be more careful. He found the feathers that needed clipping, then picked up the scissors from his lap. A few snipping sounds were followed by a few tiny feathers fluttering down onto his well-polished shoes.\par
\par
After clipping the other wing as well, he removed the handkerchief from the bird\rquote s eyes. It flapped its freshly-clipped wings a few times, then perched on his finger. It then resumed its singing as if nothing had happened.\par
It was a beautiful song.\par
