\ldblquote Goodness! Oh, my goodness! Layla, not again!\par
The frantic shouting coming from the foot of the tree almost made Layla drop the apple she had just started eating.\par
Barely catching the apple before it fell, she looked down and saw just the person she expected\emdash Chef Mona, glaring up at her with her arms crossed.\par
\ldblquote Hi, Chef Mona! Isn\rquote t the weather great today?\rdblquote  she casually said before putting the apple and her book into her bag.\par
Mona\rquote s frown deepened as she watched Layla nimbly slide down the trunk of the tree. Just then, Bill returned to the cottage.\par
\ldblquote This is all your fault, Mr. Remmer!\rdblquote  Mona shouted, clicking her tongue at Bill as he set his wheelbarrow aside. \ldblquote I told you that you needed to raise her to be a well-mannered young lady. Having raised three daughters myself, I thought you would\rquote ve listened to my advice, but you ignored it, and look at her\par
\par
now! To think, a fully-grown adult, not to mention a teacher who\rquote s supposed to be a role model for children, climbing trees!\rdblquote\par
Looking perplexed, Bill shouted back, \ldblquote Is there a law against schoolteachers climbing trees? I thought a good teacher should know how to do everything!\rdblquote\par
Ever since Layla had first come to stay at Arvis, Bill and Mona had been arguing about the proper way to raise her.\par
Layla thought about telling them that she was an adult now and they didn\rquote t need to worry about her anymore, then decided against it and hesitantly approached Bill.\par
\ldblquote Look at her!\rdblquote  Mona shouted. \ldblquote It\rquote s your fault, Bill. You should\rquote ve spanked her when she was younger. Then maybe she wouldn\rquote t have become such a tomboy!\rdblquote\par
At this frightening comment, Layla flinched and reached for her bottom. Bill also froze in place as if he were being punished for doing something wrong.\par
Mona kept nagging Bill for a short while longer, then, suddenly remembering the reason she had come over, handed them a basket full of snacks and left. Bill and Layla remained motionless for a second, then glanced at each other and burst into laughter.\par
\ldblquote I feel like I\rquote ve been spanked, just from her words,\rdblquote  she said.\par
\ldblquote This is no good, Layla. From now on, you should try not to let her find out when you\rquote re climbing trees. I\rquote m worried I\rquote m going to go deaf from all her nagging.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Sure, I\rquote ll do that for you, Uncle Bill.\rdblquote  She lifted the heavy basket and walked towards the cottage. The old bag on her shoulder rattled with each step she took.\par
\par
\ldblquote Anyway, that old bag needs to be thrown in the incinerator soon.\rdblquote  Bill had resolved to throw away the bag many times but could never bring himself to. He flashed a relieved yet bitter smile.\par
Despite Bill\rquote s worries, Layla was living life to the fullest. When the new semester started, she started working as a rookie teacher. She had made the occasional mistake, but she never felt discouraged for long. Instead, she always used her mistakes as opportunities to improve.\par
By now, she seemed to be having a lot of fun teaching at the school. Bill had worried at first since he still saw her as a child. But soon he saw that he didn\rquote t need to worry about her competence. However, he knew that she still harbored a sad wound deep inside her.\par
He suspected that her refusal to throw away the bag was because she associated it with the lost happy days of her younger years. He knew better than anyone how much Kyle meant to her. They had been best friends before she had become his first love. He had been like family to her. The pain of losing him wouldn\rquote t heal quickly. He knew that she would need more time.\par
Not yet, he thought, sliding the letter that had come from Kyle deep into his pocket.\par
Even after moving to Ratse, Kyle had sent a letter to Layla every week. The mailman had delivered these letters specifically to Bill. Bill had requested this, and the mailman had readily complied.\par
Bill knew this was cowardly and cruel of him. He knew it wasn\rquote t mature. But despite the guilt he felt towards Kyle, he considered it more important to protect Layla\rquote s feelings.\par
\ldblquote Uncle Bill!\rdblquote  she shouted from her seat on the porch, waving at him. Smiling bashfully, he walked over to her.\par
\par
They sat next to each other, sharing an apple as they enjoyed the cool breeze. The forest had taken on a majestic, autumnal color palette.\par
\ldblquote Oh, by the way,\rdblquote  she said, \ldblquote I need to see the butler about something. Would you be able to talk to him for me?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote The butler? You mean Hessen?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Yes. It\rquote s about my work at the school.\rdblquote  She pulled a handkerchief out of her apron and wiped the juice from her fingers. \ldblquote I\rquote d like to see if it would be all right for me to bring my students to Arvis\rquote s forest for an autumn field trip.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Ah, yes. Hessen will be able to ask the higher-ups and let us know if it\rquote s all right. I\rquote ll talk to him for you.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I\rquote m a little worried they\rquote ll think it\rquote s a rude request.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Rude? The duke\rquote s mother and grandmother are very generous about things like that. I\rquote m sure they\rquote ll allow it. Especially since they\rquote re sponsors of the school.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote Sponsors?\rdblquote  she asked, looking surprised. The Herhardt family sponsors the school where I teach?\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote You didn\rquote t know that?\rdblquote  he casually asked. The House of Herhardt sponsors practically every school in the district.\rdblquote\par
\ldblquote I see\'85\rdblquote  she quietly muttered. Suddenly, the duke\rquote s face flashed into her mind, so she shut her eyes to try to get rid of the image.\par
Sometimes she got the feeling that wherever she went in Karlsbar, she could never escape the name \ldblquote Herhardt.\rdblquote  Of course, it wasn\rquote t just a feeling. It was reality.\par
The King of Karlsbar-that was the nickname the people of the city had bestowed on Duke Herhardt.\par
The House of Herhardt was an aristocratic family of the highest rank, with wealth and power comparable to that of the imperial family itself. They were a\par
\par
source of pride for the people of Karlsbar and held great symbolic significance. \ldblquote What\rquote s the matter?\rdblquote  Bill asked. \ldblquote Did something happen between you and\par
the Herhardts? Is the duke\rquote s bratty fiancee bothering you again?\rdblquote\par
Startled by his question, she shook her head. \ldblquote No, not at all.\rdblquote  Once again, her thoughts were overtaken by images of the duke\rquote s face, his eyes, and the various excruciating experiences she had had with him. She didn\rquote t feel like she could tell Bill about this, though, so she simply said, \ldblquote Let\rquote s have a cup of tea, Uncle Bill.\rdblquote\par
Before he had a chance to reply, she had bolted up from her seat and hurried to the kitchen. She poured the tea into two cups, and she put some of the pastries Mona had brought onto a plate.\par
The days were getting shorter, so it was already dim inside the house, but she didn\rquote t switch on the lamp. It was almost as if she wanted to hide her worries in the dark.\par
