There\rquote s no need to rush. Such was Matthias\rquote s feeling about Layla Llewellyn. He intended to have her for himself, but he felt no need to be hasty about it.\par
\ldblquote Looks like Miss Llewellyn is coming this way,\rdblquote  said Matthias\rquote s assistant, Mark Evers, chuckling as he noticed Layla walking down Platanus Road with her students.\par
Ever since Layla had established herself as a respectable adult and schoolteacher, the residents of Arvis had started addressing her as Miss Llewellyn.\par
\par
\ldblquote They must be having their field trip today,\rdblquote  the chauffeur added.\par
Matthias\rquote s grandmother had granted permission for the children from the village school to have an autumn field trip at Arvis. His mother had also agreed that it would be fine. Such matters were traditionally up to the lady of the house, so Matthias had respected their judgment.\par
Layla was surrounded by elementary- school students as small as she had been when she first arrived at Arvis. She still enjoyed exploring the forest like a child during her free time, but in front of her students, she behaved like a mature adult.\par
Matthias let out a silent chuckle as he gazed at her through the car window. The car soon passed by Layla and the children, but the image of Layla stuck in his mind for a long while afterward.\par
Recently, messing with Layla Llewellyn had become his favorite pastime. The more ways he contrived of ingeniously tormenting her, the more lively her reactions became. Her emotions at these points were limited to embarrassment, anger, shame, and fear, but he didn\rquote t worry himself about such labels. He enjoyed seeing her become flustered, get upset, and talk back to him much more than seeing her obediently smile.\par
The previous weekend, he had run into her in the greenhouse when she was helping Bill Remmer tend to the greenhouse\rquote s flowerbeds. She had been so startled by the sight of Matthias that she had dropped the basket she was holding, and the dirt-covered flower bulbs had tumbled across the stone floor. Bill had been busy tending to another flower bed on the other end of the greenhouse and hadn\rquote t noticed the commotion.\par
Matthias had calmly approached and stood in front of Layla. When he started kicking some of the bulbs towards her, Layla had looked angrily up at\par
\par
him. She had seemed nervous, perhaps worried that someone would see, but her eyes were full of defiance.\par
He had chuckled to himself at the thought that everyone else saw her as a kind, tenderhearted woman who would never speak ill of anyone. It was a nice feeling. Having never learned to share with others, he appreciated that Layla seemed to save her anger for him alone.\par
The beautiful birds that lived in Arvis\rquote s heaven, as the greenhouse was called, had been chirping loudly. After taking a few deep breaths, Layla had quickly gathered up the bulbs, put them back in the basket, and stood up.\par
She curtsied and was about to run off when he had impulsively decided to trip her. She had staggered, losing her grip on the basket, and the bulbs had once again tumbled across the floor. But she hadn\rquote t fallen. Before she could, he had wrapped his arms around her waist to catch her.\par
Putting her hands over her mouth to stop herself from screaming, she had flailed about frantically. Even though it was well past the rose season, he had detected a hint of rose scent coming from her.\par
He had then let her go, taken a step back, and nodded towards the bulbs on the floor. She had frowned severely in response to this, her eyes full of reluctance. But she had had no choice but to follow his command.\par
Biting her lip, she had stooped down again to pick up the bulbs. Once again, he had kicked some of them towards her. Her cheeks had flushed red. It was a pretty color.\par
He would have been pleased to see her whole body flushed that color. What a nice color, he thought. My color.\par
\ldblquote What\rquote s my afternoon schedule today?\rdblquote  he asked as the car approached downtown.\par
