When he saw the bird through the window, Matthias repeated the name he had heard Layla say in such a desperate tone earlier: \ldblquote Phoebe.\rdblquote  Why would she give a mere pigeon such a lofty name? he wondered, snickering at the thought.\par
He rose from the sofa and opened the door to the balcony. A cold, damp breeze blew in from the river. As he approached the pigeon, it showed no alarm, but only kept pecking away at its feed. The docile bird had quite a different personality from its owner.\par
The sun continued to set as Matthias leaned against the railing. He shut his eyes and listened to the clear sound of the breeze. He could also hear the quiet, beautiful sound of autumn leaves rustling in the distance.\par
\par
Signs of autumn pervaded every corner of Arvis, and Layla was there too.\par
He watched her diligently pedaling her bicycle to work each weekday morning and coming home looking more tired each afternoon. He watched her use her spare time helping the gardener with his work or carrying a basket into the forest, where she gathered mounds of mushrooms and wild berries.\par
In Arvis, the beautiful world that he possessed, everything was in its right place. The perfect order of his estate brought him great satisfaction, and it helped him to forget the disillusionment that he had felt after becoming an informant to secretly help the desperate Linda Ettman stop her son\rquote s wedding.\par
It was only right, he felt, that Layla should be situated amongst Arvis\rquote s perfect order.\par
Immersed in that pleasant, intoxicating feeling of satisfaction, he opened his eyes. The pigeon, having eaten its fill, flew off in the direction of the cottage, returning to its owner.\par
Matthias was now certain that the bird would come back to the cabin\rquote s balcony tomorrow and the next day. He had no interest in the bird\rquote s feelings, but he knew plenty about how to train it.\par
To win the messenger pigeon\rquote s heart, all he had to do was provide it with ample feed at a safe location. He had learned this technique in the military. Soldiers would use messenger pigeons when there weren\rquote t good electronic communications in place or when there was a danger of electronic messages being intercepted by the enemy. Matthias was now using the same training techniques on Phoebe.\par
After Kyle had left for the capital, Phoebe had quickly realized that it was no use flying to his window anymore. Around that time, a new source of food had appeared at a safe location, so Phoebe had started flying there instead. Unlike its foolish owner, the bird was quite smart.\par
\par
As he gazed in the direction it was flying off to, Matthias suddenly frowned, as he was reminded of how Layla had looked sitting on the balcony on the day of the autumn field trip, when her smile had faded the moment their eyes had met. He was sure that she had shown the doctor\rquote s son that same smile without reserve, and this thought filled him with bitterness.\par
Ever since she was young, Layla had refrained from smiling in Matthias\rquote s presence, but she hadn\rquote t been able to hold back her tears. Thus, he had made her cry. If he couldn\rquote t make her smile, making her cry would have to do. It didn\rquote t matter to him whether she smiled or cried. If all he could give her were tears, he would make her shed tears. If he could give her pain, he would make her hurt inside.\par
But what if there was something else he could give her? Lately, he had been wondering about that. He was tired of seeing her frightened or angry. Instead, he wanted her to look only at him, occasionally pestering him in baby-talk to give him something, then erupting into a bright smile when he gave it to her.\par
He now knew what desire felt like. He desired Layla Llewellyn. He desired everything about her.\par
