Arvis was astir with preparations for the banquet that was going to be held for relatives of the Herhardt family.\par
Returning from work, Layla walked her bicycle along the edge of the mansion\rquote s driveway, which was teeming with carriages and automobiles. For days, Bill had been extremely busy preparing the flowers that would decorate the mansion. Since it was past the blooming season for flowers in the garden, he was mainly using flowers from the greenhouse and going to florists in the city for varieties of flowers that weren\rquote t being grown in the greenhouse.\par
Layla had helped him with these tasks.\par
She felt lucky to be able to spend several days busily occupied like this. It enabled her to forget about the pen she had lost and the note from the duke that she hadn\rquote t responded to. But now the work was done, and she had to return to Arvis. She slowed her pace as she approached the mansion\rquote s\par
Fortunately, the duke had made no more mention of the matter. Perhaps he hadn\rquote t yet had the opportunity to. He had also been busy for days, and\par
\par
Phoebe, with whom he had been secretly communicating, had been locked in a cage in the cottage\rquote s backyard.\par
\ldblquote It can\rquote t be,\rdblquote  Layla muttered, just as she had hundreds of times since the night when Phoebe delivered the duke\rquote s note.\par
How had he come up with the idea to train Phoebe to act as his own messenger pigeon?\par
Layla hadn\rquote t yet lived a long life, nor had she met many people, but in all her years, she had never met someone as strange and inscrutable as Matthias von Herhardt.\par
And why was he always taking others\rquote  possessions? \ldblquote Is he a kleptomaniac?\rdblquote  Layla grumbled as she kicked a pebble from the road, frowning.\par
As great as her desire to get her pen back was, her desire to avoid any encounter with the duke was at least as great. He was no longer acting mean to her, and yet her desire to avoid him felt even stronger than before. She didn\rquote t know why she felt this way, but she did.\par
She let out a deep sigh and was about to enter the estate through a side gate when she was startled by the loud sound of a car horn. She turned around and saw the Brandt family\rquote s car facing her.\par
She approached the car with stiff steps, and the back-seat window rolled down to reveal a familiar face.\par
\ldblquote It\rquote s been a while, Layla,\rdblquote  Claudine said, smiling. \ldblquote I hope you\rquote ve been well.\rdblquote  After she quickly looked Layla up and down, her smile grew brighter.\par
Layla unwittingly held her breath. She was gripped by a familiar sense of guilt, like that of a child who had done something very naughty. It was the same feeling she had felt at the end of the summer the previous year when facing Claudine on the day of her and Duke Herhardt\rquote s engagement ceremony.\par
\par
The thought that her first kiss had been stolen by Claudine\rquote s fiance made Layla feel utterly small and pathetic.\par
Looking into Claudine\rquote s eyes reminded her of the duke\rquote s soft voice and laughter and the feeling of his large, hot hands, which brought that moment back to her mind.\par
Clasping her cold, stiff hands together, she curtsied politely to Claudine as always. \ldblquote Hello, Miss Claudine.\rdblquote\par
