A week had passed since he had sent the note, but neither the messenger pigeon nor Layla had returned.\par
Staring up at the stretch of the sky into which the white pigeon had flown away, Matthias chuckled. His laughter contained no warmth. Instead, it was almost an angry laugh.\par
\ldblquote You can go now,\rdblquote  he told the servants waiting in front of the balcony door. \ldblquote There will be a car waiting in front of the cabin when it\rquote s time for\par
departure,\rdblquote  Mark Evers said before leaving with the rest of the servants.\par
Left alone on the balcony, Matthias stared into the dark water of the river for a long time. For a while, he had been able to explain it away as a result of\par
\par
her being flustered or being too busy helping the gardener with banquet preparations. But not anymore.\par
He lifted his cuff to check his watch, then walked across the balcony. There was still some time left before his afternoon meeting, and it was the weekend, so Layla was probably at the cottage.\par
He decided to assume that she was. Without hesitation, he departed. He walked along the riverside. Once he reached the leaf-covered forest path, he started walking with even longer strides.\par
Just as his regret over not having made Layla cry was growing deeper than the color of the autumn leaves, he found her. She was off to one side of the cottage\rquote s front yard, hanging laundry. She looked so peaceful and relaxed, he felt silly for having waited so restlessly for the past week.\par
He stopped in his tracks and gazed at her. She was fully engrossed in the task of unfolding and hanging sheets. With each sheet, she wringed it out in several spots, groaning as she struggled to get all the water out that she could, then spread it out and placed it on the clothesline.\par
He felt even more frustrated as he saw the look of satisfaction on her face. Look at that, he thought, as he had been thinking a lot lately. He found her sly toughness irritating but also quite entertaining. In the end, it was just annoying, though.\par
He took a step forward, and she turned her head. When she saw him, her body went as stiff as the clothesline pole next to her. Up to that point, he had found her cute, but now he had revised his opinion.\par
She turned around and started running. It took him until she had made it all the way to the far end of the yard and disappeared before he realized that she was fleeing him.\par
\par
He laughed. The way she was running away as if she had seen a ghost was ridiculous, as was her foolish presumption that it was all right for her to run away from him.\par
He deferred his judgment for the time being, however, and began to chase after her. She was running in the opposite direction from the Schulter River, towards the harvested crop field and the stream.\par
The gap between them quickly narrowed. He gradually increased his speed, while she kept stumbling as she fearfully looked over her shoulder.\par
He finally caught her under a willow tree by the stream. He had grabbed her by the shoulders, and firmly trapped her small, struggling body between himself and the tree\rquote s trunk. His hot hands clutched at her disheveled hair.\par
Looking down at her tear-filled eyes, he smiled. \ldblquote Where do you think you\rquote re going, Layla?\rdblquote\par
